/** OC siman 363 — 8 slugs, 210 keys */
export const T = {
  "biur-halacha:11:_": `But they did not lean on it, etc. — And specifically for lechi; but for a complete partition it is explained in the poskim that it helps even if they did not lean on it from erev Shabbat; and see in Peri Megadim who is uncertain how is the law regarding tzurat hapetach if on the previous Shabbat the city's people did not want to carry through it — is it permitted to rely on this on the coming Shabbat when they did not intend from erev Shabbat to lean on it; and know that even for lechi if he made it initially for lechi even if they did not lean on it from erev Shabbat it also helps [Gaon Yaakov], and all the more so for tzurat hapetach one can be lenient in this:`,
  "biur-halacha:14:_": `On the walls of the mavo — He comes only to exclude that he should not place it from outside as he concludes below; but the same if placed in the hollow is fine; and see below in seif 26 in Biur Halacha s.v. it is not permitted with korah:`,
  "biur-halacha:15:_": `Invalid — See Mishna Berurah in the name of Peri Megadim that even if the korah is 4 wide it is also invalid, for this too is because of recognition. And behold according to what we copied below in seif 26 in Biur Halacha s.v. it is not in the name of Rashba that for a korah of 4 that was fit to receive plaster it is valid even above 20 amot and not because of recognition but because the edge of the ceiling descends and closes — it is possible that in this even if it stood by itself it is permitted, and it requires study:`,
  "biur-halacha:16:_": `And therefore if he made it, etc. — See in Chiddushei Ra'ah who wrote that if it was a korah 4 wide it depends on the two answers of Tosafos Eruvin 80b: for R' Avraham's answer there, since for a korah 4 wide it need not be strong to receive a brick — as below it helps even from asheirah wood see there; and according to what Peri Megadim wrote in that seif that a reed on tzurat hapetach does not help according to R' Eliezer from asheirah wood since it cannot endure in air after it becomes ash — if so certainly here too it does not help for all opinions:`,
  "biur-halacha:17:א": `Only that it should be, etc. — See Beit Yosef who tends to say that if made of straw and reeds even if strong to receive a brick it is invalid since they do not distinguish; see there however in Ritva it is proven explicitly that in this manner it is valid even of straw and reeds, and his words were brought in Biur HaGra s.v. and some say it needs:`,
  "biur-halacha:17:ב": `Strong, etc. — See the reason in Mishna Berurah that it should be recognizable to all that it is for permanent building — so Rashi in Mishnah 13:2 and Rabbenu Yehonatan; however in Rashi 3 he explained the reason so it not be a korah removed by wind, and so appears in Peri Chadash there see there. And behold for width 4 tefachim that Shulchan Aruch ruled it need not be strong to receive a brick — study is needed if it was so thin it is removed by wind whether it helps, for according to Rashi there 3 it must be that for width 4 we are not concerned for this; but for Rashi here and Rabbenu Yehonatan one can say that even for width 4 it need not be strong to receive a brick, nevertheless it must not be so thin that it is removed by wind, and it requires study:`,
  "biur-halacha:1:_": `The Sages forbade, etc. — And regarding whether it is called reshut hayachid from the Torah to obligate one who throws from it to reshut harabbim — there is a dispute among the early authorities: for Rambam chapter 14 it is not called reshut hayachid from the Torah except with four partitions or at least three complete partitions and a lechi in a 4[-tefach] wall that is considered as a partition, and so is proven from R' Chananel in his commentary on daf 12 — see there; however according to most poskim [namely Rashi on Eruvin 12b and Tosafos in several places and Ra'avad in his hasagot and Rosh first chapter of Sukkah and Rashba in Avodat HaKodesh and in his chiddushim and Ritva] with three partitions or with two partitions and a lechi it is complete reshut hayachid from the Torah. And know there is no practical difference for this view whether the two partitions were like a gimmel or the two partitions were opposite each other — for even in this it is called reshut hayachid from the Torah since it has another lechi (so is proven from Eruvin 12b from the Gemara's difficulty there from the baraita of R' Yehuda see there); however if reshut harabbim passes between the partitions it is proven in Shabbat 6b that for all opinions this lechi does not help to make it reshut hayachid since many pass between the partitions; and see in Eruvin 12b in Tosafos s.v. itiveih and 22a s.v. drabbanan drabbanan, etc., that it appears at first glance contrary to our words; but the Gra already wondered on them from that sugya in Shabbat, and so I found in Ritva 22a in the name of Tosafos themselves that the language they do not combine reshut harabbim in this implies even from the Torah it does not help. And know further there are some early authorities who make a distinction between the views and it is brought in the Taz: where the mavo or courtyard is open to reshut harabbim, since sometimes many push into it and enter inside, there is not upon them from the Torah the name reshut hayachid, and this resolves the sugya of all roofs 94a where it appears explicitly there that whenever one wall of his courtyard adjacent to reshut harabbim was breached — if one threw from it to reshut harabbim he is exempt — see there in Ritva and in Rambam chapter 17 of Hilchot Shabbat and in Kesef Mishnah there in the name of Ramban:`,
  "biur-halacha:22:א": `That they must be close, etc. — From the plain meaning of Shulchan Aruch's words it appears that even according to this view we also require that the two korot together between them have the measure of a tefach like the first view, and so is proven for one who examines Rabbenu Yehonatan see there, and also from Ritva's words there is no proof to the contrary — for what he wrote in the second answer and a finger on this and a finger on this, etc., means it suffices that this measure be placed on the korah, but the korot themselves each are half a tefach wide [however for the first answer brought in Ritva and this is the explanation Rashba took in his chiddushim and in Avodat HaKodesh it is stated that the two korot themselves need not have the measure of a tefach because the air between them joins to this; and Eliyah Rabbah understood to harmonize the view of all early authorities who stand on the second approach that we do not require the measure of a tefach in the two korot — but in truth this is only Rashba's view, but the view of the other early authorities is not proven they hold thus, and on the contrary from the plain meaning of their language it appears to the contrary that the measure of a tefach is always needed]; and Ritva's words are brought in Biur HaGra — if so Shulchan Aruch's intent is as plain, and as we wrote; and know that although the Mechaber wrote on the second view in the name of some — it is not a lone view, for such is the view of Tosafos and Rabbenu Yehonatan and Rashba and Ritva and Rashi and Hagahot Maimoniyot — they are stricter that even within a tefach it does not help, and specifically when they touch — see Bach:`,
  "biur-halacha:22:ב": `Within a tefach — And even if together they are 4 tefachim [such as each korah holds 3/4 tefach and between them the air is 2 and a half tefachim] it also does not help according to the first view; and although there in seif 17 he ruled that for width 4 it need not be strong to receive a brick — specifically strong we do not require but fit to place on them we require [Maggid Mishneh and Shach], and even otherwise one can say that until here we do not permit as width 4 tefachim even if not strong except in actual width — then there is excellent recognition even if not strong; but not here where only through joining the air it becomes 4:`,
  "biur-halacha:25:_": `And its curvature inclines into the mavo — For if it inclines toward the outside what will it help in what we say throw chavit — behold the korah will be outside the mavo and invalid, and so in seif 4:`,
  "biur-halacha:26:א": `Spaced — See in Mishna Berurah that we go stringently, and although it is a rabbinic matter and we should go leniently in its doubt — nevertheless it is not called a doubt due to lack of knowledge [Chavot Da'at]; but if another doubt joins it is possible to be lenient [Peri Megadim]. And it is wondrous that the later authorities did not mention Rashba's words in Avodat HaKodesh that his view for a mavo is we always measure constricted see there:`,
  "biur-halacha:26:ב": `Except with tzurat hapetach — Behold in Eliyah Rabbah he brought that in width more than 10 it helps when making a pas 4 or two pasin of mashehuin like in a courtyard, and he brought a source for this from Rashba; and see in Beit Me'ir who wondered on this, for also in a courtyard itself this does not help when its opening is more than 10 as explained above in seif 2, and also in Avodat HaKodesh itself he wrote thus — and therefore what he wrote in sha'ar 1 ot 4 the mavo lacked all these, etc., rather with a pas 4, etc., does not refer to width more than 10:`,
  "biur-halacha:26:ג": `It is not permitted with korah — Meaning even if 4 wide. And if 4 wide and strong to receive plaster it is permitted and not because of recognition but because the edge of the ceiling descends and closes — as long as the opening of the mavo is not more than 10; it further appears that if it were 7 wide even if not strong it is valid since whenever it is 4 wide the eye dominates [Avodat HaKodesh and brought in Eliyah Rabbah; and in Peri Megadim he wanted to emend from 7 to 4 and it is wondrous, for 4 does not help as he wrote at the beginning of his words; and the reason for his emendation was it was difficult for him what advantage of 7 — however now that we merited Chiddushei Rashba on Eruvin published, he wrote there on 3 a proper reason on this see there]. And know further what he said it is not permitted with korah — this is if he wants to place it above on the walls as usual, so the korah is found above 20; but if he wants to place it in the hollow of the mavo he has a remedy also with korah — insert pegs in the two walls of the mavo below 20 from this side and that and place the korah on them; and what is written in seif 4 and he must place it on the walls of the mavo — comes to exclude that he should not place it from outside as he concludes there; but it need not be specifically on the walls, and so is proven at the beginning of Eruvin in Rashi s.v. yima'et he will lower, and so in Rif and Rosh and Rashba at the beginning of Avodat HaKodesh and Ritva there [although he explained there further that diminution includes what the Gemara 4b said diminish the hollow by raising the ground, but he also agrees diminution includes also lowering the korah as Rashi there see there] — all wrote he can lower the korah that was initially placed at the height of the mavo walls that was above 20 and place it in the hollow of the mavo within 20:`,
  "biur-halacha:26:ד": `But it is permitted, etc. — And all the more it is permitted with tzurat hapetach, and even if that opening was also wide more than 10 — lechi does not help for this, tzurat hapetach helps [Tosafot Shabbos in the name of Tosafos]:`,
  "biur-halacha:26:ה": `With lechi — And in Beit Me'ir he strongly questioned this law, and his view is superficial reading of Rambam's and Tur's language that just as korah does not help for this, so lechi does not help see there his reason:`,
  "biur-halacha:26:ו": `In a building a tefach wide — See in Mishna Berurah that the same with earth — so Magen Avraham, and it is according to what Bach agreed see there; and it is obvious his proof from Sukkah that it helps to diminish it with earth — and in my humble opinion there is not so compelling proof, for there one pours earth over the entire area of the sukkah and it is not so applicable to say lest it diminish; and although we rule a sukkah suffices with two complete walls and a third wall of a tefach suffices and can through trampling pour from the earth outward — nevertheless since it is a single person we are not concerned for this; but in our matter where the entire width of the earth will be a tefach and all residents of the mavo trample that place it is reasonable at first glance that even Abaye agrees we decree lest it open from its measure — and this is the reason of Rif and Rambam who mentioned diminution through korah, as R' Yitzchak wrote; but nevertheless for the law it appears we should not deviate from Magen Avraham's words that Rashi and Rashba and Razah and Or Zarua also copied that earth helps:`,
  "biur-halacha:26:ז": `Two courtyards — Whether they were on one side or a courtyard on this side and another courtyard on the opposite side; and the same if the two courtyards were on the middle closed wall that is opposite the open wall opposite it and the two sides were only walls without houses and courtyards [Darkei Moshe in the name of Or Zarua]:`,
  "biur-halacha:26:ח": `And for each courtyard two houses — And each house is not less than 4 amot [Eliyah Rabbah in the name of Avodat HaKodesh]:`,
  "biur-halacha:29:א": `A mavo whose one side, etc. — See in Mishna Berurah and it is according to Peri Chadash; and the same if the mavo had only one side toward the south built with houses and courtyards and the east and west sides were sea and refuse and the north side was repaired with lechi and korah — against reshut harabbim too it is permitted according to the first view [Beit Yosef and Eliyah Rabbah]; and know further that whether for the first view or the second stricter view there is no difference whether the mavo was open to karmelis or reshut harabbim, as in the poskim:`,
  "biur-halacha:29:ב": `And there are those who disagree and hold we are concerned, etc. — See in Mishna Berurah that for a river for all opinions we are not concerned lest a sandbar rise; and know all this if the river does not surround the city on all sides — then it is considered a partition opposite it to permit carrying in the city, and in the breached places tzurat hapetach will repair; but when it surrounds the city on all sides — behold this enclosure was made before the city was settled and it is as a rakafef more than beit se'atayim that was not enclosed for residence — forbidden to carry in it even if afterward they settled in it, as above in siman 358 [unless they extended the enclosure around the city afterward after they settled in it; and even if initially it was also enclosed and only in one place it was not 10 deep in an area slightly more than 10 amot — that is as a breach and they completed also in that place until no measure of that breach remained — it is considered as if the entire enclosure was afterward, as above in siman 358 see there]; and even if now a partition is made on this enclosure it also does not help as in 358 there unless one closes in one place in width more than 10 amot and afterward opens [Mordechai at end of siman 363 and Peri Megadim in Mishbetzot Zahav s.k. 20 see there what he further wrote on this]:`,
  "biur-halacha:2:א": `With two pasin of two mashehuin — Even though it is literally like lechi they called it pas by reason that he said first a pas 4 tefachim wide:`,
  "biur-halacha:2:ב": `A tefach from here, etc. — See in Mishna Berurah that not specifically a tefach; and although in Darkei Moshe he wrote that in what remained it is worse than placing pasin l'chatchila, and as Tosafos 10 wrote in the second answer — nevertheless for the law it appears as Beit Yosef wrote and as the first answer of Tosafos on 10 s.v. b'shnei tefachim that the Gemara did not mean specifically tefachim and mashehuin suffice, and so in Rambam chapter 15 Hilchot Shabbat it is explicit in what remained that two mashehuin suffice, and so Ritva on 10, and so I found in R' Chananel's commentary there on 10 in the sugya where the Gemara reads b'shnei mashehuin, and so Eliyah Rabbah in the name of Rashba in Avodat HaKodesh — certainly we do not reject all these great authorities because of Tosafos' words, especially since Tosafos themselves in the first answer agreed for the law with all these great authorities, and so Eliyah Rabbah ruled for the law as we wrote. [Afterward I found in Nishmat Adam that he also agreed thus from the essence of the law, and he brought that Rashi's view is also thus explicitly on 10a s.v. le'ashme'inan, etc., that a large one is permitted with a mashehu wrapping; but l'chatchila the view of Chayei Adam is to be strict in this matter like Shulchan Aruch unless in a time of need]:`,
  "biur-halacha:2:ג": `Since it is in its full — Magen Avraham wrote: it is possible he speaks of a courtyard wide in the middle and at its end it is narrow so that in its full width there is not 4 tefachim; but if the entire courtyard is less than 4 tefachim no repair is needed, end of his words; and in my humble opinion his words are correct for the law in this, for thus wrote Rashba in his chiddushim to Eruvin 13a: Ra'avad wrote: it appears to me they disagree in the tannaim on Rav Achlai [whether a mavo less than 4 tefachim needs repair] — such as the mavo is wide inside and narrows until the opening is less than 4 tefachim; but if its wall was shorter than 4 tefachim it is a exempt place and needs nothing, end of Rashba's words, and so Ritva there in the name of Ra'avad agreed to his words; and if so according to this all the novelty of Rav Achlai who is lenient in a mavo less than 4 tefachim is only in a mavo wide throughout but narrows near the opening, and we teach that even in this the Sages did not require repair; and when we challenge in the Gemara on Shmuel from Rav Achlai and answer Rav Achlai speaks of a mavo but in a courtyard we are strict even in less than 4 — it is reasonable we are strict in a courtyard only in the case Rav Achlai speaks of in a mavo, namely where throughout it is wide but at the end narrows, and in this we are strict in a courtyard since in any case throughout there is the measure of 4; but where throughout there is no place of 4 there is no reason to be strict, as Ra'avad wrote regarding a mavo that none disagree to be strict in such a case that whatever lacks 4 is an exempt place — so too one can say regarding a courtyard that it is impossible to be strict in such a case that it is an exempt place; and what Magen Avraham wrote as in seif 28 — that is what he wrote there himself in s.k. 29: he speaks where at its opening it is narrow but inside wide, and his intent there is the words of Rashba and Ra'avad, that if the inner width with its opening are equal even width of 3 tefachim nothing is needed even for the first view; and the later authorities did not contemplate well Magen Avraham's words and therefore challenged him, for a arranged sugya escaped them; and I found in Tosafot Shabbos and Machatzit HaShekel and Peri Megadim that they wrote explicitly as our words, except they did not see Rashba's and Ritva's words, and therefore did not fix nails on this; afterward I found in Beit Me'ir that he also agreed to Magen Avraham's words from Rashba and Ritva, and he also wrote that the later authorities challenged him in vain — and see below in seif 28 and what we wrote there:`,
  "biur-halacha:30:א": `One side long and one side short even if there are not, etc., 4 amot — See Mishna Berurah what we wrote that the same for less than this measure; and proof from what they said in the Gemara in Rava's statement that in a diagonal there is no recognition — it implies even 2 and 3 amot; however from Yerushalmi chapter 1 halacha 16 it is stated there in Rav Kahana's statement that if the excess was less than 3 amot even if there was a diagonal more than 10 it is permitted — and therefore the reason is since the hollow of the mavo itself does not hold more than 10 amot we are not strict on the small diagonal added through the excess up to 3 amot and we consider it as standing equal; and if so at first glance one can say that even according to what we rule as Rava that korah is because of recognition and in a diagonal there is no recognition — it is possible specifically in the manner Rav Kahana speaks of excess of less than 4 amot; but in a diagonal coming from excess of less than 3 amot it is possible there is no diagonal concern at all and there is recognition; however from Rambam's conclusion where he was silent and wrote a mavo one side long and one side short — it appears in all cases he only places opposite the short; and nevertheless it appears clear that at least one should be strict only when visible to the eye that one side is longer than its fellow; but when not visible to the eye explicitly and only through measurement we know one side is long — we are not strict on this, for this reason does not apply what Rashi explained that people of reshut harabbim will come to err. And it further appears to say that even regarding two lechayim that Chayei Adam mentioned we require they be aligned specifically opposite each other and not in a diagonal — and as I copied in Mishna Berurah — even if we are strict that a small diagonal is also called diagonal, this is specifically when visible to the eye as a diagonal; but they were not so strict that they be aligned through measurement:`,
  "biur-halacha:30:ב": `He only places the korah opposite the short — Machatzit HaShekel wrote: this is because even if he places it in a diagonal nevertheless he will not be permitted to carry even in the long place except opposite the short; but if he wants to place in a diagonal and not carry except opposite the short he is permitted, etc., see there; and see in Peri Megadim who disagrees and holds from the Gemara's language if he places opposite the long it does not help at all see there his reason, and his view is reasonable:`,
  "biur-halacha:30:ג": `He can place, etc. — See in Mishna Berurah what is written in the name of the Taz; and according to what we brought above from Yerushalmi it is proven from it at least that if he placed the korah in a diagonal in a manner that one side is drawn from its fellow only less than 3 amot — then even if its diagonal rises in the width of the mavo more than 10 amot it is permitted since the hollow of the mavo holds only 10 amot:`,
  "biur-halacha:33:_": `More than 10 amot — See in Mishna Berurah that the later authorities agreed to the first view; however according to what is explained below in 364 seif 3 in the name of responsum of Ra'ah that one should not entirely reject Rashba's view in a crooked mavo like a chet that he holds no repair is needed at all in their crookedness — it is possible in our matter one can rely entirely on Ritva's view, and at least not be stricter than this first view:`,
  "biur-halacha:36:_": `It is as a partition — See Magen Avraham what he wrote in Kesef Mishnah that for Tosafos if many pass through it they nullify the partition since it is not made by man — and I did not copy it in Mishna Berurah, for many later authorities disagree on this see what I wrote above in seif 29 in Mishna Berurah see there; and also what he wrote that perhaps for passing of many it is not called less than 600,000 — the later authorities are not pleased with this see Tosafot Shabbos:`,
  "biur-halacha:4:_": `Provided there is in it the width of a tefach — The Mechaber goes according to his view that he ruled above also regarding a courtyard that in what remained from the breach we require specifically a pas of a tefach from here and from here, and this matter is stricter than if he made pasin l'chatchila where mashehu suffices; and if so according to what we ruled above for the law from many early authorities that what remained is not stricter than if made l'chatchila and mashehu suffices — the same applies in our matter regarding a mavo that rises to the name lechi even if only a mashehu remained from the wall [provided they saw this from erev Shabbat and it is stam as if they leaned on it, as below in seif 11 see s.k. 9]; and behold in Levush it appears that even if we are lenient regarding a courtyard, here in a mavo one should be strict and require specifically a tefach; but in Tosafot Shabbos he challenged him, and so Peri Megadim remained with the difficulty, and so in Biur HaGra it is proven as we wrote that this law depends on the above:`,
  "biur-halacha:6:_": `Must be that there are not, etc., 3 tefachim — And see in Mishna Berurah what we wrote that if there are 4 tefachim between the tzurat hapetach and the wall — the view of Chayei Adam is that lechi must be placed at the wall; and see in Nishmat Adam that his reason is it is as if breached at its head; and behold he goes according to his view that he ruled in general seif 8 as Magen Avraham that if a breach of 4 tefachim at its head lechi is needed at the breach; but according to what many later authorities ruled there for the law not as Magen Avraham but that at its head no repair is needed, and like that of 34, and as we brought there in the name of Rashba and Ritva and it is permitted by majority standing over the breach — the same here in our matter since he holds tzurat hapetach is a complete partition and for this reason we permit even when distant 3 tefachim from the wall — if so the same even when distant 4 tefachim and more from the wall — for what difference is there; and although in a breach from its side where 4 tefachim of pas did not remain we are strict that repair is needed there — that is different since they shorten their path; unlike here where they do not leave the large opening and enter the small opening as in the Gemara; and even if we conclude there as Magen Avraham, it is not so comparable to our matter — for there since Magen Avraham holds korah repair does not help there to permit the mavo because it is not placed on the walls, therefore lechi must be given adjacent to the wall; unlike in our matter where there is tzurat hapetach and tzurat hapetach is a complete partition — automatically the breach is permitted by majority standing over the breach; and much study is needed on this, but l'chatchila certainly one should be concerned to be strict even at a distance of 3 tefachim:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:1:_": `Seif 1, a place that has three partitions — see in my Chiburei Sefer HaChayim what is written regarding the law of eruvin on the words of Terumat HaDeshen; and see in siman 363 in my Chiburei Laws of Orach Chayim from the year 5599 in a responsum to the holy community of Zlotshev what is written to a certain sage who did not understand our words, and I explained there with God's help, examine well; and behold regarding the law whether a river is judged as a partition see what is written in my responsum to the holy community of Lutzka briefly in my Chiburei Laws of Even HaEzer laws of gittin siman 129 incidentally and it applies here, see in the responsum siman 285 and examine; and behold regarding the law whether tzurat hapetach helps when distant three tefachim from the wall see what is written somewhat in a responsum to Yoreh De'ah laws of bekhor in my Chiburei siman 58 incidentally to the holy community of Chernovil, see there and examine; and behold regarding what requires study in the sugya in the first chapter of Eruvin daf 11 regarding the law of tzurat hapetach that one made from the side see in my Chiburei to Yoreh De'ah siman 95 what is written there on this with God's help, examine well:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:2:_": `(There in seif 11) Even if he did not make it for the sake of lechi, etc., provided they relied on it on erev Shabbat, etc. Behold I was asked regarding the law of tzurat hapetach that they erected on Shabbat a reed with ropes — whether it is permitted to carry even though they did not rely on it on erev Shabbat; behold in my opinion the matter is simple and clear that it is permitted to carry and we do not require that they relied on it — only for lechi where there is no new recognition now and new making, and behold it is as it was yesterday, therefore we require that they relied on it on erev Shabbat; but for tzurat hapetach which is recognizable that it is an opening — not to mention if they erected the lechi and ropes on it now, certainly it is permitted, for it is not applicable to say that they did not rely on it on erev Shabbat since there is here great recognition that they erected it today; also even if the lechi stood from yesterday and now they tied the ropes on it — also permitted since we see that it is an opening, we do not require that they relied; and what the Taz wrote at the end of this siman — there it is different for the rope is not placed on the projections but only above and it is not true tzurat hapetach but only to combine it in our minds in place of lechi — here since it stood here from erev Shabbat and also is not placed on it but only above, here we require that they relied on it on erev Shabbat; but where they made it anew on Shabbat, even though the rope is above, or it was already there and they placed the rope on it in a manner that it is like a true opening — in this certainly we do not require that they relied on it on erev Shabbat, and so it appears correct in my humble opinion since it was made like a true opening we do not require that they relied on it on erev Shabbat, and so it appears and examine well:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:3:_": `(There in seif 26) That which a mavo is permitted by lechi or korah, etc., and its width should not be more than ten amot precise. Behold this is a dispute in the first chapter of Eruvin — Abbaye holds that amot of a mavo and amot of a sukkah are in amot of a daughter of five, and Rava holds in amot of a daughter of six only precise; and behold the Ra'osh wrote there that the halacha follows Rava, for Abbaye's matter we establish like Rashbag and Rava's matter like all — and I wondered why he needed this proof, for behold it is explicit in the Talmud that everywhere the halacha follows Rava against Abbaye except yod ayin lamed kuf gimmel mem; and one cannot say that this means only in Torah matters and not in rabbinic matters — this is not so, for behold here Rava is lenient and if so all the more in rabbinic matters the halacha follows Rava to be lenient; and further behold in yod ayin lamed kog gimmel mem it deals with lechi standing by itself and thus it is proved that also in rabbinic matters this rule applies; also I saw there in the Ra'osh letter dalet that he discusses whether the halacha follows Rav Yosef who is Abbaye's teacher or Abbaye, and it is implied that he holds that what we rule halacha follows the later ones from Abbaye and Rava onward — Abbaye and Rava themselves are not included; and see in my Chiburei Nedarim Zerizin siman 207 seif 18 where I brought there in the name of the Bach to the contrary, and in my Chiburei Sefer HaChayim siman 258 — and it is astonishing that these words of the early ones brought in the Ra'osh here were hidden from them; requires study. Further I saw that the question I asked on the Ra'osh — why not according to the reason that the halacha is not like Abbaye because Abbaye is only like Rashbag and Rava like all, and behold otherwise Abbaye and Rava — halacha follows Rava except yod ayin lamed kuf gimmel mem — the Knesset HaGedolah sensed this and strained in it; and in my humble opinion one may say more correctly: behold Rava said these are amot and Rashi explained amot precise, and accordingly one may say that this sign of yod ayin lamed kuf gimmel mem — the ayin hints at amot or the mem from kuf gimmel mem hints at precise — and thereby the halacha follows Abbaye; and one may say that certainly here the halacha follows Abbaye and it is yod ayin lamed kuf gimmel mem from what the Talmud ruled in ed zomem in Bava Kamma and mumar eating nevelot in Sanhedrin like Abbaye — one may say the Talmud was received that the halacha follows Abbaye in yod ayin lamed kuf gimmel mem and was itself uncertain whether the ayin hints at amot; but in ed zomem the halacha is not like Abbaye, or conversely the ayin hints at ed zomem and only the mem hints at precise, and in mumar the halacha is not like Abbaye; and since it is uncertain to him we rule according to both stringently like Abbaye who is stringent in both; but always one may say that amot here is one of the signs yod ayin lamed kuf gimmel mem and especially since it is to be stringent, therefore the Ra'osh wrote that here it is reasonable that the halacha is not like Abbaye since it is like a tannaitic dispute and therefore this is not yod ayin lamed kuf gimmel mem — and it comes out well thus it appears to speak on behalf of the Ra'osh, and this is easier than their strains — examine well; and behold when I was on this it was difficult for me in what they said both Abbaye and Rava that in sukkah where it is to be stringent it was a daughter of five or it is precise — and everything since it is to be stringent; and it is difficult, for behold we say in Sukkah chapter 3 regarding R' Yehuda who says a sukkah is enjoyed only with the four species of the lulav a fortiori, and they said there the Rabbis: any law whose beginning is to be stringent and its end to be lenient — there is no law; if he did not find four species he would sit idle, etc.; if so it is proved that this stringency is a leniency; if so here too it is a leniency if he has no sukkah except with a bent side of four amot precise or height twenty precise; if so if we say it is invalid and he has no other he would sit idle; if so this stringency is a leniency — and how did he say it is to be stringent, behold it is a stringency that leads to a leniency; and granted in a mavo it is only a stringency for otherwise he would not carry in it and he has no other; and one cannot say that granted there where it speaks from Torah law — if from Torah law it is not a sukkah except with four species, then even if he has no other he is exempt from sitting in this one, therefore it is considered a stringency that leads to a leniency; but here which is only rabbinic — if so in truth if he has no other he sits in this one and therefore it is not a stringency that leads to a leniency; but if so why does the Talmud challenge that it is a leniency within four amot, and for one answer they do not hold that it speaks of most amot — it should have challenged more, for behold in height and bent side there is also leniency if he has no other whether he is obligated to sit in this one or not — then we measure in an amah of a daughter of six and perforce we must say it speaks of most amot; if so it is difficult for the one who does not hold it speaks of most amot — requires study, examine well. And see in my Chiburei Laws of Orach Chayim from the year 5601 in the first chapter laws of Chanukah siman 471 what is written there regarding korah — whatever part of it is within twenty or near twenty amah, as long as the hollow is only twenty — even if you lean it upward the lower one does not suffice and it would fall, nevertheless it is valid since in the end now it stands, it becomes like iron skewers; but in s'chach it is not so — only here if you lean it upward the lower one's shade would not be greater from itself it is invalid — not like Ran whom the Taz brought in siman 633, see there, a matter in its reason for it is good with God's help, examine well: (There) And if it was higher than twenty and he built a structure beneath the korah to reduce below twenty — a structure one tefach wide suffices in the width of the korah, etc. Behold what is written that it should be higher than twenty — the intent perforce is that it refers to the hollow, for the hollow is higher than twenty and not the korah, for behold the Shulchan Aruch ruled above that the hollow of a mavo we learned as is written explicitly in this seif, and if the hollow was higher than twenty, etc.; if so since here it deals that the hollow is more than twenty — why did he write afterward and he built a structure beneath the korah to reduce below twenty — it would be enough to reduce from more than twenty until twenty it is valid; from this it appears clearly that his intent is as the Maggid Mishneh wrote in s.k. 7 — it appears from the words of the Rif and R' Yonatan beginning of Eruvin that if initially it was above twenty and he comes to reduce it he places the korah so that it is also part of the twenty; but if it is not high above twenty only the korah — he need not reduce it for this purpose, perforce. And this that the Shulchan Aruch wrote precisely — first he wrote and if it was higher than twenty — meaning the hollow is more than twenty, for otherwise if the hollow was not more than twenty even though the korah is placed above twenty he would not need to reduce; but since the hollow is above twenty and if so he needs to reduce it — here he needs to reduce below twenty so that even twenty the hollow should not be only the korah should be within twenty; however it appears he need not reduce so much that the entire thickness of the korah should be within twenty — only enough to reduce somewhat from twenty so that somewhat of the korah is placed within twenty; and the reason appears: granted if the law were that the korah itself should be part of the twenty — then we require that the entire korah be within the twenty, for otherwise if you lean it — it would be a korah that was removed by wind and does not help; but according to us that we rule the hollow of a mavo we learned — then by law the entire korah may be above twenty, only therefore we require initially that the korah be within twenty lest he not be precise in measure and perhaps the hollow would be somewhat more than twenty and the korah invalid; therefore it is fit to be precise initially that the korah itself be within twenty and he not come to error in this — that if somewhat of the korah is placed within twenty there is no longer possibility of error that the hollow be more than twenty; and that the thickness of the korah is more than twenty we do not care, for in truth the entire korah is permitted above twenty for the hollow of a mavo we learned and it is not applicable to say it was removed; and therefore the Shulchan Aruch was precise and wrote that he built a structure to reduce below twenty and did not write how much to reduce according to the measure of the korah's thickness — perforce that with a slight reduction it suffices, and this is as we wrote that with somewhat of the korah that is within twenty it is permitted even initially. And with this is resolved what my friend the sharp rabbi R' Yisrael Bik asked to challenge the Maggid Mishneh's view from the Talmud Eruvin daf 3 side 2 — what is stated there that it challenges according to the one who says the hollow of a mavo we learned from what we learned how does he act — he places the korah from the edge of twenty and below, etc.; and why does it challenge — behold initially we require that the korah be within twenty and he did not say the hollow of a mavo we learned only for after the fact — and why does it challenge; and according to what we wrote it comes out very well, for behold Rashi explained there from the edge of twenty and below that the thickness of the korah should be within twenty — if so it challenges well: granted if the korah is part of the twenty — then we require that the entire thickness of the korah be part of the twenty; but if the hollow of a mavo we learned only initially it is fit that it be within twenty — here that if somewhat of the korah is within twenty and not the entire thickness of the korah — why require the entire thickness of the korah within the twenty; and what it answers there from the edge of twenty and above — the intent is not that until and not until is included and initially it is permitted to place the entire korah above twenty, only the intent is until and until is included that the korah should begin from the edge of twenty exactly and above so that part of the korah is placed at the edge of twenty and the length of its thickness goes above twenty — and this is exactly according to our view, examine well. However behold I examined again the language of Rabbeinu Yonatan beginning of Eruvin from which is the source of this law of the Maggid Mishneh — it is written there explicitly s.v. he places the korah, etc., that the entire thickness of the korah enters to complete twenty amah — and therefore the sharp rabbi's question remains in place; but the language of the Rif there does not bear even according to the Maggid Mishneh's view — this is the language of the Rif there how does he reduce it — he places upon it a korah from twenty and below, for where the hollow is twenty, end of his words — and it is language that has no explanation at all; and even according to the Maggid Mishneh's understanding there is no explanation that first it implies from his language that if he reduces he must reduce initially to be less than twenty and afterward he wrote for where the hollow is twenty — it implies he can make the reduction so its hollow is twenty — and his language contradicts itself; therefore it appeared clearly that a printing error fell in the Rif and it must read from twenty amah and above, for where the hollow is twenty; and also Rashi's view s.v. as much as he needs daf 4 side 2 is explicit there that enough initially to reduce so twenty hollow remains and not like the Maggid Mishneh's view. And behold to resolve Rabbeinu Yonatan's view it appeared — and also to resolve further what the sharp rabbi R' Yisrael Bik asked — why does it challenge on daf 4 it was high twenty amah and he came to reduce it how much does he reduce and it challenges as much as he needs — why does it challenge, let us say it challenges how much he reduces whether until twenty or more; but it appears certainly according to Rava himself who holds the hollow of a mavo we learned — even initially it is permitted to leave twenty of the hollow, only since Rabbah holds that the thickness of the korah is also part of the twenty — one may say this question how much does he reduce as much as he needs — Rav Yosef and Abbaye themselves who answer afterward are they themselves who asked thus, and like what Tosafos wrote in chapter 2 of Bava Metzia regarding and how much R' Yehuda in the first explanation, and in Beitzah daf 38 and in Nedarim likewise Rav Yosef and Abbaye themselves challenged thus — one may say they hold like Rabbah that with the korah it is twenty and do not hold like Rava, therefore since they and Rabbah hold thus and also for Rava the baraita that the Talmud challenges from it is difficult and must be emended — therefore the halacha follows Rava since he is later; and also since korah is rabbinic the halacha follows the lenient view — however this is specifically after the fact, but initially one should be concerned for Rabbah's view and his supporters and see that even the thickness of the korah be from the hollow of twenty in order to satisfy all views; but for Rava himself even initially we do not require that the korah be within twenty and it challenges well for Rava as in my humble opinion to explain Rabbeinu Yonatan's view; but since according to Rashi's view it is proved to the contrary and from the Rif also it is proved thus that there is a printing error in his words and it is proved he holds like Rashi — and this matter being rabbinic it appears we need not be concerned for this even initially as in my humble opinion here, examine well: Behold I said to copy here some questions necessary for practical law that I answered briefly. I was asked by the great rabbi R' Yoel Deutsch av beit din of Manistrishtsh regarding the eruv matter of the first chapter that was discussed — tzurat hapetach extended over several rooftops in a manner that the break between the middle houses — the lechi beneath the tzurat hapetach is not seen from this side and that, only the rope alone is seen — whether the eruv is valid; and this is because he saw in Tosafos Eruvin and the Ra'osh there in the section of ten of Rav — we require that the people of the mavo see tzurat hapetach, and in this case the middle ones do not see the entire tzurat hapetach. And this is what I answered: behold here there is no room for concern, for certainly it suffices that they see the tzurat hapetach above, and since they do not see the place — everyone knows there is a lechi from here and a lechi from here; and what he brought as proof from responsum of Panim Me'irot siman 144 and Sefer Mikveh HaMayim that tzurat hapetach does not help if they stand inside the house — behold this is not because it is not visible but the reason that a house is as one filled with water as is written there in Mikveh HaMayim explicitly; and what he questioned from the Talmud I settled this already in my novellae previously; and also even if the reason were because it is not visible — that is there where it is not visible at all from outside; but here since at least it is visible to some people it is no worse than visible from inside and equal from outside or conversely — it is judged as lechi and is not similar to a crooked mavo where tzurat hapetach is not seen at all. Further behold we do not make any correction in crookedness and rely that our mavo'ot have the law of courtyards as the Magen Avraham wrote siman 363 — if so the same here, there is no room for concern at all; and what he asked in the case where one lechi does not stand at the wall but near the windowsill — behold if the windowsill is not ten high it appears it does not help, for behold the main reason of the Tur is that it is a partition through which goats penetrate, and if so in the end goats can leap through there and the partition is nullified; therefore it appears fit to place it on the windowsill at the wall — and with this there is no room for concern at all, so I answered. Further I was asked regarding the law of tzurat hapetach that they erected on Shabbat a reed with ropes — whether it is permitted to carry even though they did not rely on it on erev Shabbat; and I answered: behold in my opinion the matter is simple and clear that it is permitted to carry and we do not require that they relied — only for lechi where there is no new recognition now and new making, and behold it is as it was yesterday, therefore we require that they relied on it on erev Shabbat; but for tzurat hapetach which is recognizable that it is an opening — not to mention if they erected the lechi and ropes on it now, certainly it is permitted; and what the Taz wrote at the end of this siman — there it is different; but where they made it anew on Shabbat — in this certainly we do not require that they relied on it on erev Shabbat; and it is correct in my humble opinion even though they wrote that the Peri Megadim is uncertain in this — in my opinion there is no room for concern and no place for doubt at all, and it is clear in my humble opinion since it was made like a true opening we do not require that they relied on it on erev Shabbat, examine well. Further I was asked by the rabbinical judge R' Yosef Kastinbrahn of the holy community of Kalmya recently — they came and corrected the city's eruvin in this manner: they make at both ends of the rope a wooden ring tied at each end and each ring is placed around the head of the lechi and the head of the lechi protrudes above those rings and the protrusion is visible to all, and the head of each lechi is grooved around it toward the outside from the rope so the ring can rest there and not fall down, end of the questioner's words. And I answered: he spoke well, for this is tzurat hapetach made from the side — why in his case, for the groove is outside and on the side of the rope there is no groove — here the entire rope and ring stand at the side of the lechi and not on it; only also even if the groove were on the side of the rope or grooved all around it — since the rope and ring are not on the lechi but on the ring and the ring is placed on the lechi — if so in the end the rope begins only from the side of the lechi and if so it is tzurat hapetach made from the side — what difference whether this side faces the street or this side faces the lechi opposite it; and between the lechayim also it does not help if it is at its side and not on it — therefore this is not a correction but a defect, and whoever adds detracts; and the essence is as the custom of the early ones and our custom, so I answered, examine well:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:4:_": `(Seif 29) A mavo whose one side ends at the sea, etc. See in the Taz what is written regarding frozen water that partitions are nullified and it is worse than garbage heap and is not in the category of a partition at all, as the Rambam wrote explicitly in his commentary chapter 8 of Ohalot that they melt quickly, see what is written there and examine: (There in the gloss) And there are those who disagree and hold that we are concerned lest the sea rise as a sandbar and there is no partition here. Behold the Taz expanded here in a responsum to forbid in all waters whose way is to freeze, similar to what we are concerned lest the sea rise as a sandbar — and the later rabbis in responsum of Emunat Shmuel and Chacham Tzvi and Knesset Yechezkel disagree and permit; and behold I have no time now to study this through analysis; but in brief it appears it is not similar to the concern lest the sea rise as a sandbar — for there it has no fixed time, therefore it is applicable to forbid on all Sabbaths of the year and on every Shabbat there is concern in this; but regarding freezing of water this is not common except in winter such as Tevet and Shevat and the like, but on other days and especially in summer days their way is not and it is impossible at all that they be frozen — therefore certainly we should not decree on summer days because of winter days since in summer days it is not common that they be frozen; and one cannot say we decree on summer Sabbaths because of winter — from where do we know to decree thus; and behold in truth the essence of the matter is rabbinic and it would be a decree on a decree — therefore we have only what the Sages said lest the sea rise as a sandbar — and that is where on this Shabbat itself there is this concern; but where on those Sabbaths it is not common we need not be concerned thus; one cannot say we decree on those Sabbaths because of others — from where do we have to say thus since we did not find this decree in the Talmud; and also even in winter days whose way is to be frozen and even when already frozen it appears the Taz's proofs are not proof — for all his proofs are from things that are not removed by themselves but only by a person, therefore one may say partitions are nullified; but frozen water behold is destined to melt by themselves and whatever stands by itself — therefore it is as if already done thus, therefore certainly they do not nullify partitions since it stands to melt by itself; and even though regarding irrigation we rule that frozen irrigation water is not considered irrigation water — see above laws of Netilat Yadayim siman 158 — nevertheless this is regarding it in itself, but in any case to be considered ground adjacent to be nullified on ground — in this even something that is not irrigation water if destined to be removed it does not nullify partitions; and if so here too — whatever its end is to melt by itself does not nullify a partition — thus it appears in my humble opinion to be lenient and rely on the view of those who permit, examine well; and see in my Chiburei on Orach Chayim from the year 5601 siman 409 in a responsum to the holy community of Barditchev what is written on this matter further with God's help, examine well; and further I was asked by the great sharp elder R' Yissachar av beit din of Shanstichov in the land of Poland regarding the bridges there, and thus he wrote: I ask one question regarding the bridges that great and mighty lords place and fish swim beneath them, also launderers are found there and sometimes people pass beneath the bridge, end of his question. Behold according to me there is no doubt this is not considered a partition — and what helps in this that fish swim beneath it and likewise people — this does not help except to nullify a partition through which goats penetrate, but to make a partition it does not help — for in the end since people also pass above, the public comes and nullifies the partition; and further I wonder at what he wrote regarding the ditch belonging to the country — who nullifies it — it is astonishing, as if this nullification requires that it be theirs — the intent is only whatever is breached and the public passes there the partition is nullified by itself and their intent is not needed; and behold in a partition through which goats penetrate — who nullifies the partition, and is there power in hand to nullify the partition — only by itself it is nullified; and also what he wrote regarding the ditch for garbage — I wonder at him how he did not sense, for behold regarding the sea we say lest the sea rise as a sandbar — it is proved that if the sea rises as a sandbar it nullifies the partition even though it is by itself and is not set for garbage — nevertheless if the public can pass there the partition is nullified; and likewise the Taz forbids where it can freeze in winter and the Magen Avraham who disagrees in any case forbids if the sea froze even though it belongs to the country and is not set for garbage — if so here too what difference whether it froze above or a bridge was made above and the public passes on it — whatever the public comes and nullifies the partition; and what difference whether it applies to an individual or the public or the entire country — by itself since the public penetrates it they nullify the partition. And also what he wrote regarding the sides of the bridge being coated with tar and an amaltara placed on them — he brought what is stated in Eruvin daf 11 that amaltara does not help in width — behold this too according to me is not so: one, that what is coated with tar does not have the law of amaltara — amaltara is when there are many designs and flowers; but what is coated with tar does not have the law of amaltara; and further amaltara only helps where there are partitions and lechi or korah only where it is wide more than ten in a manner that it is not like an opening — here regarding this the Talmud is uncertain whether amaltara helps in its width; but here in a bridge since there are no partitions and no korah and lechi — what relevance is amaltara here, especially according to the truth amaltara does not help in its width; and also what he wrote regarding amot — whether we require precise or imprecise — already see in the first chapter of Eruvin daf 3 that for eruvin to be stringent we require imprecise; and what the Rema wrote that it is a matter that is not applicable — he challenged from daf 5 — and one may distinguish: in my opinion there is no need for distinction, for are precise or imprecise from this uncertainty — it is not from uncertainty but thus the Sages enacted that at the beginning of the enactment they enacted everywhere to be stringent — this is its measure in imprecise and in precise; but where there is uncertainty then it is an eruv uncertainty and valid, so what I answered:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:5:_": `(There in seif 40) A courtyard enclosed by four partitions and breached at a corner like a gimmel on this side and that — if the breach is measured equally part this side and part that side it would be more than ten; but if measured diagonally the breach would be only ten or less — how do we measure this, whether its law is as breach more than ten or less; also if we measure the diagonal or equally opposite the short side to the wall opposite so it is less than the diagonal length; and this is what I answered: behold in my opinion the matter is clear that we measure the diagonal and it is proved in the Talmud Eruvin daf 8 from which is the source of seif 40 here — Rav Kahana said etc. a mavo one side long and one short — less than four amot he places the korah diagonally; four amot he does not place the korah except opposite the short etc.; Rav Kahana said since you heard Kahana's teaching I will say a matter: that which you said he places the korah diagonally — we only said when its diagonal is not more than ten; but if its diagonal has more than four amot he does not place except opposite the short etc.; and Rashi explained but if its diagonal has more than ten — its opening is found wide more than ten for behold all beneath the korah is opening — see there; if so it is proved from this that we measure diagonally and even if equally opposite the short it is only ten — we measure only diagonally; and if so it is proved we measure diagonally and even if equally opposite the short it is only ten; and one cannot say it is different there for it is only regarding using beneath the korah — this is not so for behold Taz wrote here that also regarding inside the mavo it is forbidden to use if it is more than ten diagonally — it is proved also regarding inside the mavo we measure diagonally; and if it is more than ten the korah does not help; if so it is proved also regarding inside the mavo we measure diagonally; thus it appeared initially; however when we examine this it is not so for there it deals entirely if he wishes to place the korah diagonally since he wishes to use diagonally and make the opening diagonally as an opening — then we measure the measure as it is diagonally since he wishes now to use the long place and places the korah thus since it equals an opening diagonally — here we measure as it is in width diagonally; but if he wishes to place the korah opposite the short equally — we truly measure the opening as its width equally; and if so likewise regarding courtyard it appears since we rule its breach is in ten amot and in less than ten amot no correction is needed — it appears likewise here and as the law in mavo through korah so the law in courtyard from its own side — to use opposite the long is forbidden if it is diagonally more than ten; but opposite the short it is permitted to use even though diagonally more since equally there are not ten amot — thus it appears correct and clear for practical law:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:6:_": `(There in the Rama gloss seif 36) and with this the mound counts as a partition — see in Magen Avraham what he wrote that it is possible it is not called penetrated by the public less than sixty myriads as written in siman 345 seif 7; and I question for behold from Rashi and Magen Avraham below siman 365 s.k. 4 what he wrote that not penetrated by the public means such as where there is excrement or mud and filth that the public's way is not to pass there — it implies explicitly that specifically where the place is not fit to pass from itself — this is not called penetrated by the public; but wherever the place is fit from itself for the public to pass even if the public did not actually pass — it is called penetrated by the public; and all the more if the public actually penetrated it only that sixty myriads did not pass — it is called penetrated by the public since the impediment is not from the place; and behold the main proof of Magen Avraham is from siman 345 that Rashi holds there is no reshut ha-rabbim less than sixty myriads and this is Rashi's approach; and from Rashi's words it is proved to the contrary that we do not require sixty myriads — for otherwise he should have explained plainly that the public did not pass meaning sixty myriads did not pass; and perforce we do not require passage of sixty myriads only whatever is fit to pass is called penetrated by the public; and it is possible Magen Avraham distinguishes between this law and there — there it deals with mavo corrected with lechi and korah since the concern there is lest they abandon the large opening and enter the small opening and nullify the mavo's correction — here whatever opening is fit for the public to pass there is this decree — therefore we require specifically excrement and mud etc.; but where the city is enclosed by partitions wall and the like that is truly reshut ha-yachid and was not permitted by the Sages to nullify the path from Torah law of reshut ha-yachid — it is not nullified until the public actually penetrated and specifically sixty myriads; however this is not to examine Tur in this siman that the early ones wrote that the mound rises in place of lechi — it implies also with the mound it is because of lechi and automatically whatever is fit to be penetrated by the public the lechi is nullified as written above and it is proved from the poskim cited not like Magen Avraham — examine well; and behold the measure of road width for penetrated by the public we require that it be more than ten amot; but in less than ten amot there is no concern as proved below in siman 365 and in the Talmud source of the law there; and indeed from Shulchan Aruch's wording there that wrote its measure — it implies the intent is ten tefachim everywhere that it wrote ten — for regarding amot it always wrote ten amot or stam ten feminine; but ten masculine always remembers regarding tefachim — nevertheless perforce Shulchan Aruch was not precise in wording and there the intent is on ten amot as proved in the Talmud that the matter depends on opening measure or not and opening measure is always in ten amot; and perforce the Mechaber was not precise in his wording; and if so for mound we require that the place penetrated by the public be more than ten amot; and at least it appears not like Magen Avraham; and here we do not require sixty myriads and it is not similar to the law in siman 345 — there it speaks regarding actual Torah law of reshut ha-rabbim therefore Rashi holds we require sixty myriads; but here for our law we do not require sixty myriads — for less than sixty myriads is not reshut ha-rabbim but at least karmelit; and rabbinically we do not require sixty myriads and even in less they nullify the partition; also one may say Rashi's intent there is specifically where there are not sixty myriads at all then it is not reshut ha-rabbim; but here where there are sixty myriads even though on that road sixty myriads did not penetrate — as long as in the body of the city there are sixty myriads and on that mound the public penetrated — the partition is nullified; also one may say here it is different for behold if there were no mound they would be forbidden to carry there and would be judged as public — only through the mound they come to make it reshut ha-rabbim — whatever public penetrates it in measure that if these public were in a place where there is no mound they would forbid one another — likewise here the partition is nullified and it has law of penetrated by the public and penetration of the public works at least to make mound partitions as if they do not exist; and if there were no mound there certainly forbidden — likewise here forbidden; unlike Rashi's case where it is not because of partition that he permits — only that in less than sixty myriads it is not reshut ha-rabbim — here permitted in less than sixty myriads; but whatever without the partition would be forbidden — only through the partition they are permitted — whatever public penetrates fit to forbid if there were no mound or partition — also now they nullify the partition; and so it appears in my humble opinion correct — examine well:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:10:_": `[10] [Levush] If he kneaded, etc. So Beit Yosef wrote — and I wonder it was hidden from him the mishnah at the end of Temurah and Yoreh De'ah siman 244 that ashes of asherah wood are forbidden in benefit — if so one must scatter them to the wind like the wood itself (Taz); and see siman 506 seif 3 — requires study; also it appears to me mitzvot are not for benefit were given, and asherah wood itself is forbidden and stands to be burned, and ashes stand to be scattered to the wind — nevertheless:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:11:_": `[11] [Levush] First form, second form, third form, fourth form. For Chokhmat Shlomo the third form does not exist, for he wrote inside that for everyone it does not exist (Melo L'Yom Tov); and see Taz and Nachalat Tzvi:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:12:_": `[12] [Levush] A tree that was planted, etc. And the same law applies if it grew by itself (Avodat HaKodesh):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:13:_": `[13] As if they leaned, etc. It implies that if explicitly they did not lean on it — such as they did not carry on the past Shabbat through this lechi — it is not a lechi (Magen Avraham); and it appears to me specifically when they did not carry on the past Shabbat at all, for there was no other lechi there:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:14:_": `[14] [Levush] For the breach is greater, etc. If the side is more than four and we are uncertain whether it stands in the partition on half or more than the breach — if it is not as breached it is permitted, for a doubt in their words is to be lenient; and further there are two sides to be lenient — perhaps the standing is greater; and if you say it is not greater — one may say perhaps like Avodat HaKodesh daf 5:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:15:_": `[15] And equal from inside, etc. But in Tosafos daf 9 and Rosh they proved that in this case it is not a lechi, for it is not called visible from outside except to those standing behind the mavo; but in truth Beit Yosef does not explain thus for Tosafos and Rosh; and nevertheless Chokhmat Shlomo explained thus, and so it appears (Melo L'Yom Tov):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:16:_": `[16] [Levush] For such is the law in every lechi, etc. It is difficult from siman 365 seif 4; and one must say here he speaks open to karmelit — requires study; and in Shulchan Aruch Magen Avraham wrote that the other side is shorter than this, and in the Gemara he speaks where the other side is also long — until here; and at first glance in every lechi its other side is short and even so it is permitted — requires study:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:17:_": `[17] [Levush] And if it is wide four amot, etc. And the same law if he paved with lechayim less less than three — the paving extends four amot; and specifically when the outer lechi does not protrude at all; but when it protrudes somewhat outside the mavo it is permitted. If they were distant from each other three tefachim — one may use until the outer end of the outer lechi; and if each lechi stands, etc. (Avodat HaKodesh daf 6):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:18:_": `[18] If he drove in, etc. Specifically when he drove within the thickness of the wall and they protrude outward; but adjacent to the wall it is permitted by reason of lechi (Olat Shabbat):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:19:_": `[19] To receive, etc. Meaning if the mavo is wide ten amot it must receive eighteen bricks which is nine amot as written in seif 25 (Avodat HaKodesh). Beit Yosef and Bach wrote: if he made a reed and reeds even if fit to receive a brick it is forbidden since most reeds and reeds are not fit — a mavo open from two sides that was corrected on one side with tzurat hapetach and on one side the two sides of the mavo were not equal but one short and one long — lechi suffices (R' Levi ben Chaviv section 6):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:1:_": `[1] [Levush] It is not reshut ha-rabbim, etc. And regarding when one throws from reshut ha-yachid to reshut ha-rabbim — Taz distinguishes between open to reshut ha-rabbim where the public presses within it that he is not liable — see there; and Bach and Magen Avraham imply that in any case he is liable; and see beginning of siman 345:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:20:_": `[20] A tefach and not in their length three tefachim, etc. For then the korah is as lavud as written in seif 25; and likewise if there is a tefach it is as a korah — see there:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:21:_": `[21] There are those who say that it is required, etc. So ruled Rambam and Semag, and so ruled in Avodat HaKodesh; and in Shiltei Giborim it implies this is also Rif's view:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:22:_": `[22] And it does not touch, etc. Even if distant from the two walls — such as he drove any amount of reed in the middle of the mavo to place korah on it — for we say lavud from two winds (first chapter of Eruvin):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:23:_": `[23] And there are those who say that it is required, etc. So Beit Yosef and Shulchan Aruch wrote; and in my humble opinion those who say do not disagree with the first explanation — for if both have width of a tefach it is permitted whenever between them is less than three; but when both have only width of half a tefach then between them should be only a tefach so that one can place on them a brick which is a tefach and a half — then one finger rests on each korah; and even though a korah needs only a tefach — here where in the two korot themselves there is not a tefach and a tefach between them — two korot are required; and so appears to me the explanation of Tur and R' Yehonatan; and so is proven in Avodat HaKodesh daf 5 — and it is clear; also Bach and the later authorities did not address this; and what Bach was stringent that we require specifically that both have width of a tefach and touch one another literally — this does not appear so in the poskim; and likewise Nachalat Tzvi disagrees with him:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:24:_": `[24] Within a tefach. And even if together they are four — if the air between them is four tefachim it does not help, for in any case it is not fit to place a brick on them — unlike in seif 14 where it is fit only that they are not strong (Magen Avraham); and it appears to me there is a scribe's error in his words and one must say if the air between them is more than a tefach it does not help — understand well; however I already wrote to be lenient whenever both have width of a tefach if between them is not three tefachim:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:25:_": `[25] And there are those who disqualify, etc. And we set it stringently (Bach):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:26:_": `[26] A korah wide, etc. Explanation: wide ten tefachim — since it hangs down ten tefachim it leaves the category of korah like a mat, and it is a partition beneath which goats break through and it is forbidden as Rashba wrote — it teaches us it is permitted since the korah is visible and is not covered by a mat — there is korah on it. And what he wrote even if it extends below ten, etc. — meaning one might have said since it hangs below ten from the ground there is no law of korah on it as he concludes. And what Taz wondered on Rashba from seif 12 — it appears to me there is no difficulty, for in a partition more recognition is required than for lechi:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:27:_": `[27] Spaced, etc. And in the latter part precise — then we go stringently:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:28:_": `[28] Except with tzurat hapetach, etc. And Rambam holds that less than four amot its law is as a courtyard; and it is possible he did not mean tzurat hapetach specifically (Magen Avraham); and it appears to me also when wide more than ten amot and above its law is as a courtyard; and Shulchan Aruch and Levush relied on the amah that wrote at the end of the seif or with a pas, etc., or with tzurat hapetach; and so appears in Avodat HaKodesh daf 4; also Magen Avraham omitted what is written in Shulchan Aruch seif 32:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:29:_": `[29] [Levush] That the eye does not rule, etc. And if the korah is wide four and fit to receive plaster it is permitted and not because of recognition but the ceiling descends and closes as long as the opening of the mavo is not more than ten; and so appears to me that if it were wide seven even if not fit it is valid — for whenever its width is four the eye rules over it (Avodat HaKodesh section 4):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:2:_": `[2] [Levush] Mashehu from here, etc. In Shulchan Aruch he wrote a tefach from here — for the remainder of the wall is less than a pas with mashehu, and this is the second answer of Tosafos Eruvin daf 10; and Levush's view appears like the first answer there that even in a wall mashehu suffices; and so appears in Avodat HaKodesh daf 6; and Rashba is worthy to rely on his ruling; and not like Bach who was stringent even with pasin at a tefach and relied on R' Yehonatan — but it is not so, for even R' Yehonatan spoke only regarding a wall; and also regarding a wall one may say he did not mean it literally, as Beit Yosef wrote that mashehu suffices:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:30:_": `[30] Building, etc. And the same with earth; and one must beware when trampled by feet that it not diminish from the measure:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:31:_": `[31] Until they are open, etc. Rama's gloss was omitted — and even all courtyards open to each other, and there are those who disagree — until here; and perhaps the reason is because Beit Yosef ruled like those who disagree, and stam is according to his explanation that they are not open to each other (Melo L'Yom Tov); and one may wonder on one as great as he that he did not see at the end of this seif that Levush wrote Rama's words as they are also for practical law primarily like the first view as Bach wrote, and likewise regarding houses open to each other; and in Avodat HaKodesh daf 4 — even if each house and house made eruv, there must be in it four amot by four amot or more:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:32:_": `[32] That its length is greater, etc. And even all that it is (Avodat HaKodesh). Which is its length of the mavo — the way of its entrance; and what is not the way of its entrance refers to its width, even though its width is greater than its length (there). The gloss that Rama wrote here — Levush wrote at the end of this siman; and Melo L'Yom Tov erred in this and thought he omitted it to expand, and it is for nothing:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:33:_": `[33] That there is not in it four, etc. Explanation: in its opening even if inside it is wide four (Beit Yosef); and so appears from R' Yitzchak daf 92 — R' Yosef wrote daf 88 — and specifically in a mavo; but in a courtyard always correction is required; and see s.k. 3:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:34:_": `[34] [Levush] That one of its sides, etc. Explanation: its bank is ten high; and specifically when it is adjacent immediately to the head of the mavo; but if the river is distant from the houses ten amot correction is required, for that place is breached; and likewise regarding a garbage heap he speaks when it is ten high; and it appears in Beit Yosef that even if one side is sea and one side garbage heap and one side closed and one side open to reshut ha-rabbim — with lechi or korah it is also permitted according to this view; and Levush set one of its sides — it appears to include even those who say that even so correction is required. Emunat Shmuel siman 40 wrote: a city with a ditch ten deep and four wide surrounding it is as a partition:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:35:_": `[35] And there are those who say, etc. And so is the main ruling; and so Bach ruled; and Magen Avraham wrote specifically regarding the sea, but regarding other rivers we are not concerned lest a sandbar rise unless its way is to dry in summer greatly; and if they freeze in rainy season the partition is nullified and it is forbidden to carry; and Taz expanded that since their way is to freeze they are forbidden forever; and in the Talmud he speaks of a sea whose way is not to freeze; and see Magen Avraham siman 365 s.k. 9:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:36:_": `[36] [Levush] And they would carry from the mavo, etc. This is the wording of Melo L'Yom Tov — so Beit Yosef wrote in the name of the Maggid; but Rashi explained they would carry in the mavo itself; and Beit Yosef and Levush omitted Rashi's explanation; and it appears to me the Maggid does not explain that the entire side is sea and also not in width more than ten — therefore he was not concerned to carry in the mavo itself — until here. And it appears to me the Maggid's view that even if dry land slope remains in the depths that is partition as written at the end of this siman — nevertheless it is forbidden lest they carry from mavo to karmelit; and so appears in Magen Avraham; except that what he wrote that for the Maggid tzurat hapetach or lechi or korah does not help — only a complete partition; and in my humble opinion it helps since there is recognition they will not come to carry:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:3:_": `[3] There is not in the breach four tefachim, etc. And it is possible he speaks when the courtyard is wide in the middle and at its end it is short so that in its full width it is not four tefachim wide; but if the entire courtyard is less than four tefachim no correction is required as written in seif 21 (Magen Avraham); and it is not clear, for there he speaks of a mavo but in a courtyard correction is required as explained in Eruvin daf 12; and so appears in Mordechai and Agudah and Hagahot Alfasi there. For tzurat hapetach see siman 362 seif 10:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:4:_": `[4] [Levush] All that it is. Even less than a finger (Beit Yosef):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:5:_": `[5] If a person tied, etc. He speaks where they did not inform him — if so he will leave from there — therefore it requires being tied (Magen Avraham); and see siman 362 s.k. 11:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:6:_": `[6] [Levush] And specifically in correction, etc. And Melo L'Yom Tov answered that two winds are different; and see s.k. 2:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:7:_": `[7] Cannot stand, etc. And even if the wind does not knock it down but only shakes it as explained at the beginning of siman 362 (Magen Avraham):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:8:_": `[8] Distant, etc. For the air comes, etc. — therefore if the lechi is wider than three it is permitted since the standing exceeds the breach (Riaz); but the view of Tosafos is that even so it is forbidden (Magen Avraham) — requires study:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:9:_": `[9] [Levush] Therefore, etc. And Taz disagrees with Rama that in Terumat HaDeshen it implies specifically with tzurat hapetach that one needs only one reed from here and one reed suffices with all that it is, and not with lechi; and there is no difficulty, for Beit Yosef also holds that with lechi all that it is suffices as above; and Melo L'Yom Tov wrote that Beit Yosef's view is that in every lechi all that it is suffices — nevertheless with smeared plaster he is uncertain; and it appears to me that for Rama it is obvious to him from that which broken fragments — until here, in She'erit Knesset HaGedolah it is explained that they practice even with lechi that one may be more lenient with plaster smearing on lechi than with tzurat hapetach, and they practice to be lenient in both; and it is obvious the plaster should be somewhat thick like the thread of a cloak. Stones protruding from the fence one above the other less than three constitute a lechi when between all of them there is height of ten tefachim (Avodat HaKodesh):`,
  "magen-avraham:11:א": `Even if he did not make it. For lechi is by reason of partition, and a partition standing by itself is valid:`,
  "magen-avraham:11:ב": `As if they leaned on it. Even though they did not lean on it explicitly (Hagahot Alfasi) — it appears that if explicitly they did not lean — such as they did not carry on the previous Shabbat through this lechi — it is not a lechi, and so is in Rashba whom Rabbenu Yosef brought:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:א": `4 amot. For 4 amot is the measure of a mavo and there is no recognition — even though lechi is by reason of partition, nevertheless some recognition is required; but if the mavo is 8 amot wide it is permitted by standing as breached:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:ב": `He placed it. But leaning on it does not help, not as Rabbenu Yosef, and so too in Taz in the name of a great one:`,
  "magen-avraham:13:א": `The lechi that extends. As written in the gloss: a mavo paved with lechayim less than less than 3, and the paving is 4 amot in the length of the mavo — another lechi is needed to permit the entire mavo, end of his words; nevertheless if visible from outside and equal inside, or the reverse, it is judged as lechi — see in the Gemara and Rashi:`,
  "magen-avraham:13:ב": `With its end. For the other side is shorter than this, and see s.k. 30 and siman 365 seif 4; and in the Gemara he speaks where his other side is also long, as explained in the difficulty above:`,
  "magen-avraham:13:ג": `And it is forbidden to use. Even if placed for lechi (Tosafos):`,
  "magen-avraham:15:_": `From erev Shabbat it is invalid. For korah is by reason of recognition, and when he did not make it for that purpose there is no recognition:`,
  "magen-avraham:17:_": `To receive a brick. And if the mavo is 10 amot wide it must receive 18 bricks which is 9 amot, and with two mashehuin it suffices for the length of the korah, and from its two ends we say lavud as written in seif 21, and so too:`,
  "magen-avraham:21:_": `And it does not touch. And even if distant from the two walls we say lavud:`,
  "magen-avraham:22:_": `Within a tefach. And even if together they are 4 tefachim — if the air between them is more than a tefach it does not help, for in any case it is not fit to place a brick on them, unlike in seif 17 where it is fit only that they are not strong, as explained above, not as I understood:`,
  "magen-avraham:23:_": `And there are those who disqualify. For he holds as the first view in seif 22:`,
  "magen-avraham:24:_": `And it extends somewhat, etc. Meaning it extends downward somewhat more than 10 from where it begins — if so it would be a partition and invalid both as korah and as partition too, for a partition is suspended — it teaches us it is valid; against Bach:`,
  "magen-avraham:25:_": `Valid when they are. For we say chavit and lavud — even though for a korah of a tefach even if higher than 3 we say chavit, here is different since korah is over a mavo (Tosafos):`,
  "magen-avraham:26:א": `Spaced. The measure of spacing is more than constricted — half a finger per amah (Maggid Mishneh, Peri Megadim):`,
  "magen-avraham:26:ב": `Except with tzurat hapetach. And Rambam wrote that less than 4 amot its law is as a courtyard, and it is possible he did not mean tzurat hapetach specifically:`,
  "magen-avraham:26:ג": `In a wide building. And the same with earth, and one must be careful when trampled by feet that it not diminish from the measure:`,
  "magen-avraham:26:ד": `Two courtyards, etc. For whenever there is not abundance of residents they use it discreetly and require more partition (Beit Yosef, Rashba), and if so it is possible to be lenient in our mavo'ot since there is abundance of residents, and it is possible they did not distinguish in a mavo:`,
  "magen-avraham:26:ה": `And there are those who disagree. Since they can make eruv together through the openings they are considered as one courtyard, and the same regarding houses open to each other:`,
  "magen-avraham:26:ו": `The law of courtyards. Since most cities are surrounded by a wall in settlement and afterward enclosed — one should apply the law of a courtyard and repair the mavo'ot (between Jews and gentiles) as one who comes to divide a courtyard into 3 and 4 domains if there were small paths in the courtyard between house and house requiring tzurat hapetach or two pasin of two mashehuin as in Hagahot Maimoniyot in the name of Maharam, end of his words Terumat HaDeshen — and if the mavo is not 10 amot wide, two pasin of mashehu suffice, and so Mahariv in his laws siman 18: in Hagahot Maimoniyot Maharam explained that our mavo'ot need only two lechayim for they are only removal of residents and do not need tzurat hapetach; nevertheless it appears our mavo'ot are not in their category as open to reshut harabbim to require tzurat hapetach in their crookedness as an crooked mavo — there he speaks of open to reshut harabbim, end of his words; and it is obvious he speaks in what is surrounded by a wall, for he wrote it is only removal of residents; but in unwalled cities tzurat hapetach is needed as written siman 364 regarding open to karmelis see seif 3 that even in its crookedness repair is needed, for there is no difference between reshut harabbim and karmelis, and so Beit Yosef there in the name of responsum of Ran, and so Mishna Berurah siman 392 — except that Mahariv's words were inclined to another view see there, and it requires study that I did not see practiced thus, and therefore it appears to me that since stam Rama wrote all our mavo'ot have the law of courtyards — it implies even without a surrounding wall, since houses and courtyards are not open into them they have the law of a courtyard, and in a courtyard there is no difference between crooked and straight — only one repair at its head is needed; nevertheless for this that is called mavo, if it has no wall tzurat hapetach is needed since they are close to reshut harabbim as Semak wrote; but its crooked part is not close to reshut harabbim and does not need repair, end of his words in the gloss; and see siman 364 that on the second side lechi suffices, and in Levush Aharon: and Maharil in responsum 160 wrote our cities where gentiles dwell within have the law of karmelis, and even utensils that rested within are forbidden to carry outside 4 amot, etc., see there, and so in his derashot; but we have only Shulchan Aruch's words who wrote siman 357 and siman 365 that they have the law of courtyards, and if breaches were made in the wall see siman 365 seif 3 and siman 372:`,
  "magen-avraham:26:ז": `Two reeds. On the ground, but the walls of the mavo are not counted in place of the reeds — for otherwise why was a korah a tefach wide needed, for in all that it is there is tzurat hapetach; rather reeds must be placed, and one can say what he wrote in Sukkah that one places a reed on the walls and need not a reed from here — see siman 631 seif 3; and it is possible this is a mere stringency to make tzurat hapetach, and it appears to me if there is a protruding pillar in the wall it is judged as a reed, for it is no worse than lechi in seif 11:`,
  "magen-avraham:28:_": `That there is not in its width, etc. Meaning its opening is not 3 wide even if inside it is very wide:`,
  "magen-avraham:29:א": `A mavo whose side, etc. It appears here that the sea is considered a partition — and it requires study, for Tosafos 22b wrote: and one might say one cannot make reshut hayachid since mountains surround it, and in Do not make reshut hayachid that surrounds it is a river Diglat — and some say a partition not made by man is not considered a partition and many come and nullify their partition, end of his words; see end of this siman, and one must say here he speaks where ships do not pass there to the port, for there the sea itself is a partition, and the same for other waters where ships do not pass — they themselves are a partition as written siman 356 and so in the Gemara 24b and Tosafos one does not make, etc., that the river itself is a partition, and so in Aruch entry Ozel; and per what is written siman 358 specifically when the riverbank is 10 deep, and so Rashi and Rivash here — but if the riverbank is a slope and when one walks the slope is still not 10 high it is not a partition, as written in this siman:`,
  "magen-avraham:29:ב": `Lest the sea rise with a sandbar. For such is the way of the sea that it shortens and becomes land the width of a parsah and partitions are nullified and they also carry in mavo'ot as at first (Rashi); and in Maggid Mishneh it appears that even if the sea shortens nevertheless a slope 10 deep remains as a partition, except the concern is lest they carry from the mavo to the place where the sea dried which is karmelis — even if surrounded by the sea's partition — for it was not enclosed for residence as written siman 365 seif 3; but when the sea exists there is no concern since the sea is not carried together with the mavo, end of his words; and if so for us complete partitions are needed on the sea side, and tzurat hapetach or lechi or korah does not help as Ameimar did who ruled against Rashi's view, and Tur holds as Rashi's view — if so repair of the rest of the mavo suffices; and it appears from Rashi's and Rosh's and Tur's words that regarding other rivers we are not concerned lest a sandbar rise, as in Tosafos 24 see there; and know that in any case the river must be literally adjacent to the head of the mavo, but if distant from the houses 10 amot repair is needed for that place is breached, and so in Tosafos 24 in the words let him make tzurat hapetach, etc., and in the words let him make lechi, etc., see there: Constantinople even though breached to the sea in several places more than 10 — it is as closed (R' Yehuda the Castilian 94, and so seif 92) see siman 415 seif 6; and in Rivash siman 415 he wrote: a mavo open to karmelis needs tzurat hapetach, and that one whose side was sea was not open at both ends for it had 3 partitions except on two sides there was a small breach on one side to the sea and on one side to refuse, and the sea bank was 10 deep, etc., end of his words; but the implication of the poskim appears that even fully open to the sea is permitted, and above siman 364; and if so in our rivers whose way is to dry in summer the 4th [season] one should forbid see siman 365 seif 3: thus the Yerushalmi 18:4 — mavo'ot open to the sea are as open to a valley, and the law of the alley of R' Chiya — he need not tzurat hapetach for it is as collected 10 from within 3; know that if they freeze in rainy season the partition is nullified and it is forbidden to carry:`,
  "magen-avraham:2:_": `There is not in the breach 4 tefachim. It is possible he speaks of a courtyard wide in the middle and at the end it is narrow so that in its full width there is not 4 tefachim; but if the entire courtyard is less than 4 tefachim no repair is needed as written in seif 28:`,
  "magen-avraham:30:_": `One side long. And even lechayim do not help against the long side, as in Beit Yosef, and so R' Levi Chaviv siman 66, and Bach wrote that lechayim help:`,
  "magen-avraham:31:א": `And they did not make. Since they did not make and cannot carry in the mavo they do not forbid the inner part:`,
  "magen-avraham:31:ב": `Two pasin. For even one courtyard can divide thus (Rosh), and a city surrounded by a wall with doors fit to be locked except at one opening — that opening does not detract from lechi or korah and the entire city is like one mavo that has lechi at its head and one does not make eruv for half of it; and if there is a breach the qualification is nullified (and see siman 365 seif 2) and one may qualify half and leave half (Beit Yosef siman 364 in the name of Rivash):`,
  "magen-avraham:33:א": `A pas 4 wide. But less than that does not help even if each side is less than 10, for the air of this side and that side comes and nullifies it as written seif 4:`,
  "magen-avraham:33:ב": `And some say there is not, etc. Beit Yosef questioned this, for we rule in siman 364 seif 3 that even if there is not 10 in its crookedness it is as open — one must say the Tur holds it is different here since both openings are on one side he will not go around the pas, as Ritva wrote see there; but regarding more than 10 the Tur does not hold as Ritva, and nevertheless the main thing is as the early authorities, as in Bach:`,
  "magen-avraham:34:א": `A pas wide, etc. And lechi is also needed for the mavo, for a pas does not help since it is distant 3 from the wall (Tosafos, and so R' Yitzchak), and see s.k. 6:`,
  "magen-avraham:34:ב": `That they not leave. But stam we are not concerned they will go through a small opening that does not diminish walking thus, unlike in siman 365 where it is from the side and they shorten their path we are concerned for this (Gemara):`,
  "magen-avraham:36:א": `And with this he began, etc. And if the entire city is surrounded by a mound and afterward they built houses in it — it is forbidden as written siman 358 seif 2 see seif 8:`,
  "magen-avraham:36:ב": `It is as a partition. And per what is written in seif 29 in the name of Tosafos — one must say that many do not pass through it, for otherwise many come and nullify the partition since it is not made by man; and it is possible it is not called many unless less than 600,000, as written siman 358 seif 6, and so is the view of the poskim who were silent on this:`,
  "magen-avraham:3:א": `All that it is. Even less than a finger (Beit Yosef, Bach):`,
  "magen-avraham:3:ב": `One tied a person there. And it requires study — for in siman 362 seif 5 it says regarding a person he need not be tied, and one must say he speaks where they did not inform him that they placed it for lechi — if so he will certainly leave and not stand there all day, therefore it requires being tied:`,
  "magen-avraham:5:_": `Cannot stand. And even if the wind does not knock it down but only shakes it as written at the beginning of siman 362: thus an incident where Yom Tov fell on the 6th day and the eruv was lost on the 5th day, and Maharik permitted because on Yom Tov one must also make eruv for things without any need for Yom Tov — for we say since it was permitted for Yom Tov, etc., end of his words; and it appears to me one should not rely on this — nevertheless if two days of Yom Tov fell on the 5th and 6th days, then the 1st day is chol and it is not applicable to say since it was permitted it was permitted; rather even the first Yom Tov on the 6th day is forbidden, for perhaps Yom Tov and Shabbat are two sanctities as written siman 416 seif 2; see siman 365 seif 7:`,
  "magen-avraham:6:_": `Distant from the wall. For the air of this side comes and nullifies it (R' Yitzchak) — therefore if the lechi is wider than 3 it is permitted since the standing exceeds the breach (Shach, Riaz) see siman 362 seif 9; but the view of Tosafos is that even so it is forbidden see seif 34:`,
  "magen-avraham:7:_": `Even fragments. Stones protruding from the fence one above the other less than 3 constitute a lechi (Gemara 4:15):`,
  "magen-avraham:8:_": `From asheirah wood. And we do not say crushing diminishes its measure as written siman 316, for lechi also requires some measure of height and width, since it requires only a small measure the Sages were not strict (Tosafos 4:80), and see there further, and see in Maggid Mishneh and siman 372 seif 15:`,
  "peri-megadim:10:_": `That there is not — Taz: behold those who place korah in Eruvin 17a — dispute there; and it can be explained plainly — he drove two pegs straight on top of the wall at its head and placed the korah on them; and the diagram the Mechaber wrote is what is in the Gemara 9a — Rav Ashi said two pegs bent — and that is below in seif 25; and the diagram in the Gemara there see there as Rashi explained — it teaches us throw down and lavud; Magen Avraham letter 21; and it will be explained there; and on this the Mechaber wrote here a novelty — even though the essence of leaning the korah on the pegs is toward the side of the mavo — nevertheless some say it is not required in those who support the korah except to receive the korah alone; and see Beit Yosef:`,
  "peri-megadim:11:_": `That it requires — Taz: Beit Yosef challenged — plainly reed is invalid, for it cannot receive a brick see there; and Taz: reed is not broken in weaving if a brick was placed on it except that it bent and folded; and it came below ten literally — therefore invalid; what the Mechaber wrote in seif 20 bent Eruvin 14a see there:`,
  "peri-megadim:12:_": `Within — Taz: Magen Avraham 18; and see Beit Yosef — it appears for the reason that here there is no measure — two korot touching one another literally are required; if separated even less than a tefach — forbidden, for here there is no measure; but Taz explains that there is no measure means one cannot lean half a brick on them:`,
  "peri-megadim:13:_": `Somewhat — Taz and Magen Avraham letter 20; and the principle is — korah that one placed below nineteen or above twenty amot is invalid; however a very thick and wide korah that one placed and its upper head above nineteen — even though it is wide and reaches the ground below nineteen — it is valid, since the upper head is above nineteen; for here it is better than seif 20 — we view what is within nineteen as if they are not — it teaches us the Mechaber: if it is very wide and has approximately nineteen in it and is placed entirely above nineteen — it has not left the category of korah even though it has width of nineteen like a partition; for above nineteen there is no law of partition — even if its width reaches below nineteen and more than 3 tefachim to the ground — if so it left the category of korah and is disqualified; and partition it is not, for more than 3 tefachim to the ground; and on this Rashba disagrees — it teaches us the Mechaber it is valid; and what he wrote from 362 letter 7 — if made for lechi even protruding four amot valid in lechi; and what he wrote korah — see letter 27 — in lechi recognition like korah is not required see there. And know that in seif 62 in lechi drawn along the width of the mavo — it is difficult from seif 4 — who is worse in a wall in a fourth wind? One may say since the mavo was initially breached in its entirety and he made correction with lechi four amot long and not with intent — it is not permitted thereby until it stands with intent:`,
  "peri-megadim:14:_": `Spaced — Taz in all the laws — even of scribes here we go stringently; wherever nineteen is required we require spaced; and wherever disqualified in more than ten amot and the like — also precise disqualifies; and see Olat Shabbat; and one may say there is still one doubt — whether they combine for two pasin — requires study:`,
  "peri-megadim:15:_": `It is not — Taz and see letter 29 — korah four tefachim wide descends and closes in air wider than ten; and also "the eye rules over it" see there; and it appears an error in what he wrote seven — only four see there; and nevertheless in 366 in Magen Avraham section 5 — four tefachim even in more than ten helps — requires study. And lechi helps at height more than twenty, for it is partition; and see Bach seif 64 — lechi equal inside and outside is not judged because of lechi unless it protrudes somewhat higher than the walls of the mavo; and such a case — all that height above five amot there is no recognition even though lechi is partition nevertheless recognition is also required see there; and Tosafos Shabbos letter 53 brought it; and it will be explained there in letter 19 further:`,
  "peri-megadim:16:_": `Requires — Taz: Rashi Eruvin 5a s.v. Rav Yosef; and see Levush; and behold it implies building width of a tefach like the width of the korah, and according to the entire width of the mavo that building; and in carving according to the entire width of the mavo in length four amot; and siman 631 in Magen Avraham section 8 — less than 3 tefachim to the side is valid; and if so here one may say whatever is less than 3 tefachim in the width of the mavo for two walls and less than 3 tefachim in length four amot to the wall — it should be valid; and if we say carving as if the air were closed from the side — if so here too; and it depends on the dispute whether carving is as actual substance etc. — see what is written in Taz section 1; and in the dispute of Tur in 631 see there — understand:`,
  "peri-megadim:17:_": `And there is — Taz: in the corrected edition nevertheless as fitting; and Magen Avraham letter 27 on this; and it will be explained there:`,
  "peri-megadim:18:_": `And there is — Taz as above in seif 2 line 4 — or two lines mashehuyin on each side one of them. And behold our towns that are not surrounded by a wall that have breaches between house and house — they are not in their entirety according to the majority, only a breach more than ten amot; if so it is impossible with line four, only with tzurat hapetach as in seif 2 see there; and a breach that is like ten amot — no correction is required at all, since it is not breached in its entirety as in seif 2 see there; and it will be explained in letter 19 some correction for our towns and the law of tzurat hapetach and lechi and line four; and here there is no place to expand:`,
  "peri-megadim:19:א": `And therefore — Taz: siman 362 in Taz section 4 and Magen Avraham letter 19 — implies one must beware in such a case because of the viewer; and what he wrote that we conclude in the Gemara Eruvin 11b regarding a dome — and what he wrote that the roof must not interrupt; and all the more letter 64 challenged him from Eruvin 11b dome proves the interruptor does not ruin; and Yoreh De'ah 287 section 3 challenged him see there; behold in Yoreh De'ah there in letter 2 — for Rambam another approach in Eruvin and Yoma 11b; see Be'er Heitev there; and nevertheless Peri Chadash from what he wrote here that for Rambam whatever is not in a flat mezuzah nineteen high — if so the circle — which will you make of them mezuzah and which the arch, or all mezuzah there is no lintel, or all lintel there is no height nineteen; and he does not hold carving; unlike when there is in the flat twenty-nine — then the entire arch is considered lintel; and what is in the Gemara Eruvin and Yoma 11b "without width of four" — and there is to carve four tefachim — a novelty he said even without width of four in the circle, all that can carve four tefachim Rambam holds carving; but if there is no carving four tefachim — Rabbenu Tam also agrees there is no carving to complete height ten; and for the Rabbis in every case all that has mezuzah height straight nineteen is obligated even though there is no width of four at all; and when there is not height nineteen straight even though there is width of four in the circle itself — nevertheless which will you make mezuzah and which lintel; and one may say how did he say support that tzurat hapetach in reeds is not required for Rambam; and for Rashi properly from height nineteen etc.; unlike for Rambam height nineteen of the circle immediately becomes lintel; and there is to reject and say from Rambam he brings proof to say carving and as if touching actually we do not say; and therefore one may say the Rabbis do not hold so; and in truth Rif and Ra'osh — halacha is like Rabbenu that taught thus — we publicize not like Rambam's explanation; and nevertheless Peri Chadash from what he wrote here nineteen — halacha is like Rabbenu — he did not conclude "taught thus"; and with this one may say Taz's words what requires a hole in the roof — understand; and there is no place to expand:`,
  "peri-megadim:19:ב": `The simpleton said who writes on the law of tzurat hapetach — there is to investigate. (1) The upper reed if it is above twenty amot and air of twenty between the reeds and that which is tied to a roof very high — what is the law. (2) Tzurat hapetach that he distanced from the side 3 tefachim — what is the law. (3) Partitions that are not height nineteen — whether tzurat hapetach helps. (4) Tzurat hapetach made from the side in 362 — whether specifically from the side — for it is inserted between reed and reed and carved somewhat in the reeds as craftsmen do — even though the reeds protrude above also, it is possible it is permitted; behold in Bach it implies permitted in tzurat hapetach even though the upper reed is above twenty between it and the reeds beneath; and all the more on this; and tzurat hapetach that he distanced 3 tefachim from the wall of the reeds — Magen Avraham section 5 — for Tosafos implies forbidden; and also in Biur Halacha one may say forbidden; and all the more in the name of Tevu'at Shor from this; and partitions that are not height nineteen — in Shulchan Aruch here in section 26 it is explained that tzurat hapetach helps for low partitions less than nineteen — whenever tzurat hapetach is height nineteen; and requires study in siman 345 that all that are low less than nineteen is karmelit; and all the more letter 51; and in Rambam section 17 from what he wrote fourteen — they disagreed on him and hold all whose partitions are less than nineteen tzurat hapetach does not help; and see Beit Yosef what he wrote and so wrote Rashba — it must be; and it is not Rashba's view; and requires study — one may say what is written in Shulchan Aruch "less than nineteen" is at the place of the opening — for all listed there are three partitions height nineteen, otherwise it is like karmelit as a house whose interior is not nineteen in siman 345 — requires study; and siman 362 in Taz letter 4 what he wrote from the side; and see Hagahot Alfasi Eruvin 11 — and not that it be inserted between both in its middle — implies it must rest on the head of the reeds; however driving a nail in the heads of the reeds and wrapping rope on them helps as above; and what Taz wrote wrappings protrude until nineteen near the ground — it appears proper to emend 3 tefachim near the ground; see seif 10 in lechi; and all the more letter 11 and letter 64 — wrappings not for tzurat hapetach, for it is petachei Shammai Eruvin 11a petachei Shammai; and Yoreh De'ah 287 see there in Taz — requires study; and therefore wrappings in a wooden wall called Wängel and the like — requires study in this; and tzurat hapetach that between reed and reed is not four tefachim — all the more 362 letter 25 for Rambam tzurat hapetach in less than width four tefachim helps; and for Tur we require four like mezuzah; and nevertheless section 17 section 20; and see seif 28 in any case all that the opening of the mavo is not width four amot — for Rambam tzurat hapetach helps; and for Ra'osh correction is not required except when very wide and made many tzurat hapetach that are not width four tefachim, or open such a case; and there is to see if open and the opening is not width four tefachim — whether its law is as open:`,
  "peri-megadim:19:ג": `And know that dome is mentioned in 362 seif 12 — and for Rambam and Rashi like dispute in mezuzah — likewise here; and one may say for the Rabbis in dome all that is less than 3 tefachim of the mezuzah of seven amot — why do we not say carving as in siman 631; and Magen Avraham section 8 there whether the carving reaches the wall, or we say as one who fills until the groove — if so:`,
  "peri-megadim:19:ד": `If tzurat hapetach was ruined on Shabbat — whether we say since it was permitted it was permitted — Magen Avraham letter 4; and what I wrote there; and in siman 374 implies specifically courtyard to courtyard — but to another domain we do not say since it was permitted it was permitted; and Magen Avraham letter 27; and it will be explained there in 374 from this; and if on Yom Tov it was ruined — whether we say since it was permitted where it is fit to say since it was permitted — it will also be explained there; and here there is no place to expand:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:א": `There is Taz nevertheless section 17 from chapter 5 of Shabbat law 9 see there, and siman 345 in Magen Avraham section 1 — dispute of Rambam and other poskim; and nevertheless lechi is because of partition d'oraisa and korah is because of recognition, and Eruvin 12 — a mavo they qualified with lechi — one who throws into it is liable, with korah exempt according to Rambam; it will be explained with three partitions and to explain poskim with two partitions; and Eruvin section 17 here, and nevertheless there lechi is d'oraisa and korah is the issue; and see Levush here; and practical difference to disqualify testimony or a mumar who desecrates Shabbat d'oraisa is a mumar — so it is; however some say we have no reshut ha-rabbim — siman 345 in Taz section 6:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ב": `The simpleton said who writes on the law of lavud and tzurat hapetach — I have thoughts on the matter and I set them forth here with God's help. Know that lavud is nevertheless, and even two lavud above and below, and even in length we say lavud; and in explanation of lavud — not that which they say as closed; nevertheless for lechi distant from the wall less than 3 tefachim Magen Avraham section 5 — rather even if we say that in any case air is as closed, as in seif 35 and three ropes etc. siman 362 — behold there is height ten; Rashi Sukkah 4b s.v. less than etc. that they extend etc.; and behold in lavud nevertheless it is a complete partition for all its matters and from the conditions of lavud in 14 seif 1 — Rambam wrote in the gloss that one cannot say lavud to be stringent see there; in Magen Avraham siman 372 letter 9 he expanded on this and brought s.v. Eruvin 10 see there; and in siman 632 — or section 5 — explanation of what Tur wrote that we do not say lavud to be stringent, that it is like adjacent — do not err to explain his words as lavud like adjacent, for we hold lavud as extending and as closed by here; rather to say lavud entirely to be stringent like closed; and in invalid s'chach we do not say except like closed extending and not in invalid s'chach; but in beit 19 invalid s'chach and another 19 invalid between them air less than 3 tefachim — I have to doubt whether lavud whether we also say as adjacent and invalid, or only as extending and closed and we say as closed in valid see there; and see Levush; and behold if we say lavud as actual substance to say as if it is not — the matter inquired in Sukkah 4b and below in chapter 5 Sukkah 632 seif 10 — he carved up to ten less than 3 tefachim to the wall — we say lavud; and Rashi: the carving as if it reaches the wall — behold lavud as actual substance; and one may say this is dispute in Tur there Magen Avraham there section 8 whether valid from carving to wall — one may say lavud as if the partition seals the air over the carving, not the ground between them as removed — understand. And responsum Chatam Sofer section 59 in small gardens wrote lavud as actual substance we do not say; and proof invalid s'chach that between them is valid less than 3 tefachim — we do not say lavud as if the valid were removed in actual substance see there; and this is in 632 seif 4 specifically air between them; and one may say that whenever there is valid between them entirely we do not say lavud to be stringent — see Levush there. And what he wrote there proof from Eruvin 25a — partition adjacent to partition less than 3 tefachim as lavud — therefore lavud to be stringent — one may say not from lavud theory but it is annulled and not considered; Rashi there made as if; and there are poskim like Rav Shimi bar Ashi even though we do not hold so. And in siman 345 less than 3 tefachim lavud implies even in actual substance — see there in urine and feces and in that of Shabbat 7 threw and did not land on anything less than 3 tefachim etc.; and therefore lechi distant less than 3 tefachim from the wall and between them a board of asherah wood — some say invalid, for it is actual substance; and even though burned it counts for measure nevertheless it is actual substance that we do not say lavud; and Magen Avraham here section 5 and 631 Taz section 2 — if so if asherah wood stands there it is worse still; and all the more here letter 8 and Magen Avraham 626 section 6. And behold lavud is a complete partition nevertheless to obligate one who throws there, and also for leniency; and from rabbanan it is called inferior as in siman 304 Magen Avraham section 2; and one may say the stringency of two without eruv, or the reverse — for all that have two and eruv one may say even if he drove stakes less than 3 tefachim and one reed on top of them alone for eruv — it is an excellent partition, and even more than beit se'atayim; and in encirclement and eruv alone like those fifteen attached to pegs in four bundles — requires study. Siman 358 section 6 in Levush — lavud to reduce air more than 3 tefachim we do not say; and see there:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ג": `Tzurat hapetach — its law is explained in 362 seif 10; and here section 26 — and it implies even if made from asherah wood it is valid, for the measure of lechi is not required; and what requires d'oraisa — hard and ashes are not fit for that — nevertheless permitted; and all the more letter 9 he brought s.v. on ladder of asherah; and it will be explained below. And all the more in siman 362 letter 25 whether d'oraisa is required in tzurat hapetach — and it will be explained there in Taz letter 19. And know that I am in doubt in tzurat hapetach whether it is partition d'oraisa to obligate one who throws within when driving four pegs and upon them tzurat hapetach — then tzurat hapetach helps to permit karmelit; and a breach more than ten amot — nevertheless it is partition from something — tzurat hapetach helps; and siman 631 sections 62 and 63 imply tzurat hapetach alone does not help for sukkah; and in section 26 he drove four pegs — implies even if he placed a reed above them it does not help; and Magen Avraham letter 9 — any mashehu is partition in sukkah helps etc. — it appears tzurat hapetach does not help except for karmelit and the like — not to be complete reshut ha-yachid through tzurat hapetach; and requires much study. Siman 358 in water — does not make a petach — one may say tzurat hapetach does not help there whenever wider than 3 tefachim; and siman 361 in Magen Avraham section 4; and all the more here in tzurat hapetach whether we require width of 4 tefachim or even less — and it will be explained there in Taz letter 19 further:`,
  "peri-megadim:20:_": `Perhaps — Taz: in a city surrounded by a river high whose banks are nineteen — it is partition; and they acquired from the lord as in siman 382 — not to forbid gentiles — he raised it is forbidden; and he brought Gemara Eruvin 8a — prohibition he did not say because partitions stand; and he raised even on other days of the year in summer forbidden to carry in the city on the river see there; behold the river — in winter when the waters freeze and it is possible more frequent than the sea sandbar of thirty-nine for one view — unlike here; however if there is a place of slope it is permitted etc.; and in Magen Avraham 41 in the name of nevertheless implies even if there is a place of slope it is forbidden see there; and Taz holds even for nevertheless all that there is a place of slope — earth that collects nineteen within four amot is permitted; and Magen Avraham in winter days forbids and in summer days perhaps permits; and it will be explained there; however in other rivers for sandbar we are not concerned; and therefore he permitted in beit tel etc. Magen Avraham. And behold in concern of sandbar one may say rabbinically thirty-nine likewise unlike frozen and many pass there — it is actual reshut ha-rabbim — there is to decree more; and see in siman 345 Taz section 6 and Magen Avraham section 7 that they are lenient to say nowadays it is karmelit; and nevertheless with the view of Rambam here one may say. And behold one may say what Rashi wrote sandbar like width of a parsah as nevertheless explained and they would carry — and there in the sea and it is enclosed not for dwelling as Magen Avraham wrote letter 41; and Marimar who ruled in Uzla — one may say he holds properly we are not concerned for sandbar lest they carry from mavo to sandbar, for they were not accustomed to that and it would not come to that; unlike within the city they would carry as initially — we are concerned properly etc.; and Sura had no slope nineteen along four amot and he was concerned properly lest they carry in the city as initially — and he ruled in Uzla; and therefore how it will be in any case when there is slope it is permitted; and this that Taz brought nevertheless's explanation and Rashi like width of parsah — for a specific intent he brought thus and as stated; and it will be explained further in Magen Avraham. And see responsum Knesset Yechezkel siman 2 and other simanim — he expanded here see there; and Magen Avraham 365 letter 9; and see here letter 35; and regarding a city surrounded by a deep ditch nineteen high and width four tefachim — it is complete partition to permit the city — responsum Chatam Sofer section 5 section 6; and see siman 374 in Magen Avraham letter 29 — all that the width of the ditch is less than four tefachim it is easy to step over and is not partition for the city; and if there are bridges wider than nineteen — tzurat hapetach specifically is required; and it will be explained in 365 in Magen Avraham; and here there is no place to expand; and know that a river surrounding or a tel surrounding a city is karpef more than beit se'atayim — Magen Avraham letter 38 letter 20 — rather one may say when there are bridges wider than ten amot the partition around the city is annulled; and afterward when he made tzurat hapetach at the place of the bridge as in siman 358 section 62 — it is enclosed except for the reason of roofing; and therefore in a place where the bridge is not width ten amot in any case forbidden to carry in the city; and it will be explained further in Magen Avraham:`,
  "peri-megadim:21:_": `But — Taz: Eruvin 8b implies even if he wishes to use only opposite the short side — nevertheless they do not place the korah diagonally lest they err and use opposite the long against the long; and also there is not so much recognition when placed diagonally; and further that korah over the walls of the mavo is required, and in length it is not over the walls of the mavo — forbidden to use there; and this is what Rava said — he places only opposite the short, and he did not say he uses only opposite the short — if so for Tosafos section 1 s.v. "places" — for Rava why do I need the reason that there is no recognition — it should suffice that there are no partitions; and see Tosafos Shabbos they brought it. And what Taz wrote section 15 — it appears within the mavo even diagonally permitted — namely when in the diagonal there is not more than ten; and you saw that more than ten — even for Rav Kahana forbidden — likewise for Rava within the mavo such a case (and the words "except diagonally" must be emended — and the reading: rather requires ten precise and not more) — and with this what Rif wrote more than ten — all agree dispute is only within the mavo as stated; and see Beit Yosef; and Taz wrote that from the Mechaber's and Tur's wording it implies within the mavo even if there is more than ten in the diagonal permitted to place the korah diagonally — for otherwise he should have said first within the mavo permitted diagonally and afterward written tzurat hapetach permitted opposite long and short; and nevertheless they are not equal — for korah within the mavo like the width of the opening of the mavo more than ten amot certainly does not help even when the walls of the mavo are equal; and tzurat hapetach helps only when more than ten is in the diagonal that within the mavo with korah is permitted; and all the more. And two lechayim he wrote there is no doubt at all — certainly it permits only opposite the short when placing them at the end of the mavo — not like Bach; and Magen Avraham letter 32; and what he wrote that opposite it is reshut ha-rabbim — one may say lechi is partition and there are three partitions from the long side and the breach in a fourth wind is not in its entirety; and all the more for karmelit such a case; and nevertheless one may say even open to karmelit two lechayim diagonally do not help — for the short lechi is as if partition there opposite it straight, and further there is no permission at all opposite the long lechi; and see letter 23:`,
  "peri-megadim:22:_": `If — Taz: behold he placed lechi in the middle of the mavo — namely in the middle of the wall and adjacent to the wall less than 3 tefachim as explained; and all that the outer ones also made lechi for the remaining mavo and they can make eruv together — then they forbid one another even though the outer ones have no treading of foot on the inner ones — for it is like a city of an individual that became of the many since they were accustomed to make eruv together; see Beit Yosef in the name of Ra'osh; and it appears to me even if they did so initially — each placed one lechi for itself and they were never accustomed to make eruv together — nevertheless since most mavo'ot are accustomed thus to make eruv together — all the mavo is likewise; and so too somewhat in Levush; and if the outer ones have no lechi — they do not forbid the inner ones since the outer ones are not fit to make eruv; and it appears even if the outer ones have treading of foot on the inner ones such a case where they did not make lechi — they do not forbid the inner ones; unlike when the residents divided with pasin or tzurat hapetach and the outer ones did not make eruv — if the outer ones have treading of foot they forbid the inner ones; and if they have no treading of foot they do not forbid; see Beit Yosef; and Tosafos Shabbos; and what the Mechaber wrote in 362 — and all this when there is qualification of mavo — namely he placed lechi in half the mavo and not outside; and nevertheless section 17 section 20 refers above — for they also made outer ones and made eruv — the mavo combines together whenever there are between them all two courtyards — permitted; but in tzurat hapetach two pasin — I am in doubt if in the outer part there are not two courtyards whether they are permitted — requires study:`,
  "peri-megadim:23:_": `But — Taz: one courtyard itself can divide with tzurat hapetach or two pasin; see Beit Yosef; and Magen Avraham; and see Eruvin 14b — half a mavo has half a mavo — plainly; and one may say perhaps it came to teach us that the outer ones do not forbid it when they did not make lechi even though they have treading of foot; and one may say it is preferable to the second; and one may say let us say it has only half as initially that requires two courtyards etc.; and one may say:`,
  "peri-megadim:24:_": `And when he places — Taz: and even though it is breached more than standing in a fourth wind and also the air of this side and that side is greater — since there is length four amot in the pas it is like a mavo whose measure is mavo length — and therefore it is like two mavo'ot; unlike in 364 the pas is not width four amot except because it reduces the opening of the mavo from twenty amot; and in Magen Avraham it will be explained further:`,
  "peri-megadim:25:_": `All — Taz: and see seif 26 laws of conditions of mavo:`,
  "peri-megadim:26:_": `And what — Taz: in 364 bent mavo like a gimmel or like a chet is like open even though there is not width ten amot between reshut ha-rabbim and karmelit or in its bends; and here Ra'osh wrote specifically when there is more than ten amot from the pas until the middle wall (until three partitions and a fourth wind open in the east he places in the east pas four one; and if from the pas until the western wall more than ten amot etc. — tzurat hapetach is required as in 364); and Taz wrote that here it is not literally like bent mavo; and one may say we view as if the place from the pas goes straight until the western wall — and two mavo'ot are on the side of this pas and on the side of the two they also go equally and straight — and in neither is open; unlike when there is more than ten amot from the pas until the western wall — if so there is no opening between the middle mavo and the mavo'ot at the sides — therefore then it is like bent mavo; and therefore what Rambam wrote and some say specifically when there are ten amot — it is one view; rather more than ten amot:`,
  "peri-megadim:27:_": `And he shall make — Taz: Eruvin 10b; and see siman 362 — breached more than standing in the entire circumference is not partition; unlike in one wind — if the air of this side and that side is greater it annuls it; all that is less than four amot — if not, it does not annul it:`,
  "peri-megadim:28:א": `From within — Taz: all that has height nineteen and collects from within four amot — it is partition; and it is as if it does not exist; and likewise for enclosing the city so it is; and it will be explained in 365 in Magen Avraham section 9 and in other places it will be explained further:`,
  "peri-megadim:28:ב": `The poor man said I will record here words and the names mentioned in this siman with some explanation. Pasin: lechayim: tzurat hapetach: korah: (1) Pasin are (a) pas of the courtyard in seif 2 — four tefachim or two pasin mashehu from here and from here; and in what remains in the wall four require a tefach from here and from here. (b) Two pas width four amot in a mavo wider than twenty — the mavo explained here requires four amot specifically to be considered a mavo by itself; and then the air of this side and that side does not annul it; and even breached more than standing in a fourth wind does not annul it. (c) Pas four amot standing in the width of the mavo — it has no law of lechi by itself; but with intent it is lechi; but standing in the length of the wall of the mavo — even if placed for lechi it is annulled from the law of lechi even though recognizable as in seif 12 and 13 see there. (d) Pas in wells — even though breached more than standing — nevertheless since on each side an amah from here and from there it is properly partition nevertheless see Tosafos beginning of making pasin 17b and nevertheless the Merciful One concluded thus; and Eruvin section 17 section 27; and there is pas (5) in 365 that suffices with pas four tefachim such a case see there in Magen Avraham section 1 see there. (2) Three lechayim are: (a) lechi that permits a mavo breached in its entirety in ten amot — width has no measure and height nineteen; and valid from asherah wood; and should not distance from the wall of the mavo 3 tefachim as explained in seif 3 etc. I said to record here what is written on the side to the portion of Vayishlach see there and I will bury them under these terebinths in Shechem and in the name of our rabbi Rabbenu Bachya — gentile law is not in burial but scattering specifically is required; and in Rambam chapter 8 from gentiles how annulment — he crumbles or burns and casts into the sea; and see Yoreh De'ah siman 346 seif 64; and at the end of Temurah that it is not considered gentile that is not burned and buried — for scattering specifically is required and not burial; and there he said asherah wood forever forbidden — difficult, for in any case it is not burned; and one may say ashes are buried — perhaps rabbinically mashehu therefore so it is at the end of chapter 8 foundations of the Torah; and Pnei Yehoshua Pesachim unlike asherah wood mashehu — nevertheless fully adheres to your hand and one is lashed for benefit; and see Even HaEzer laws of kiddushin. And Taz letter 5; and in Tosafos Eruvin chapter 2 s.v. "but" — practical difference between the answers — reed above two reeds in tzurat hapetach — for answer one permitted and for two forbidden; and korah that he said there metal measure — somewhat difficult for answer two and for what Ran wrote in the name of Baal HaMaor Rosh Hashanah 29 — rabbinic mitzvot we say mitzvot are for benefit — difficult for lechi from asherah wood how permitted mashehu; or four partitions are required — nevertheless it is karmelit; and we hold that even rabbinically not for benefit as I wrote elsewhere. (b) Two lechayim in seif 4 in width four in the wall require a tefach — for in the wall itself there is no recognition in mashehu. (c) Lechi standing by itself in seif 11 — valid even a planted tree even less than a tefach; and siman 362 and 13. Tzurat hapetach — reed above asherah wood I explained above in disqualification of korah its law is explained. And lechi partition and one who throws to mavo is liable; and korah recognition and one who throws to mavo is exempt; and there was no time now to study properly — perhaps I will merit in the coming simanim to explain further:`,
  "peri-megadim:2:_": `Courtyard — Taz: the difference between mavo and courtyard — siman 362 and mavo — for it is permitted with lechi or korah many conditions are required; but in courtyard, whenever its length is greater than its width — with this condition alone lechi or korah suffices; siman 362; and therefore he set the difference of square in courtyard:`,
  "peri-megadim:3:_": `Tefach — Taz: explanation of the Mechaber's words that he retracted here from what he wrote in Beit Yosef and holds that in what remains in the wall we require a tefach from here and from here, as in seif 4 in lechi in the wall — 4 are required a tefach; and in Levush he wrote here two mashehuyin; and likewise Be'er Heitev section 5 implies his view is so; but in truth requires study, for Beit Yosef in his long book brought Tosafos 10a s.v. "and two" and brought it in seif 4 in lechi — and from there he learned it; and Tosafos in courtyard wrote their words — if so, courtyard also requires two tefachim; and see in section 2; and again I saw in Tosafos Shabbos letter 4 he stood on this:`,
  "peri-megadim:4:_": `Therefore — Taz: his view in tzurat hapetach — plaster smearing and coating on the wall helps, and it should be somewhat thick and protruding, and a reed above; but lechi alone without tzurat hapetach — plaster smearing and protrusion do not help, for measure of something is required. And in Ateret Zekenim letter 9 he wrote for Beit Yosef — in lechi too any amount see there; Magen Avraham section 2; and in responsum Teshuvot HaDavar siman 74 it is explained so — lechi according to one view like a finger does not help with plaster smearing; but for the Mechaber in section 63 it helps; and in section 26 requires study; and it will be explained there:`,
  "peri-megadim:5:_": `From wood — Taz: all that stands to be scattered is as scattered; except Tosafos section 1 — the essence according to the Mechaber in section 62 in any case all the more; and a finger is not required in plainness; and height ten tefachim — no question; see Be'er Heitev letter 70; and see Tosafos Shabbos letter 13 — he brought Tosafos Eruvin chapter 2 s.v. "but" see there. And behold it implies anything mashehu requires measure like shofar and lulav in its length even though in width there is no measure — invalid like cut pieces etc.; and so too from rabbanan requires measure in length alone, like high nineteen they were not stringent; and korah from rabbanan requires width of a tefach — even from rabbanan disqualifies; and lulav on 19 — second in the mitzvah they were stringent see there in Tosafos. However there is to doubt in a matter mashehu that requires measure in height — whether nevertheless invalid in cut pieces, or only from rabbanan; and practical difference — two partitions and lechi of asherah wood such a case — Magen Avraham section 1; and in section 2 one may say mashehu (1) and all the more here letter 13; and see Even HaEzer 124 and Choshen Mishpat section 1; and in section 2 one may say — the question of Choshen Mishpat how he wrote the get in 39 implies entirely — one may say all that stands to be burned is as burned only for stringency and not for leniency see there. And siman 506 section 63 and 639 section 63 — mitzvot are not for benefit were given even in rabbinic mitzvah; and such a case is considered a mitzvah even though movable from words of Torah; unlike in 72 section 66 — ladder of asherah forbidden to climb see there; and Magen Avraham letter 7; and it will be explained there:`,
  "peri-megadim:6:_": `Between — Taz in Eruvin 70b — and it appears "equal outside" — all agree it is lechi; and "appears outside and equal inside" — there is dispute; and the halacha is they are lechayim; and "between between" that the Mechaber set — for according to the majority, between-between is this one, and a fortiori this one as what Semag wrote in Choshen Mishpat 72 letter 8; and if so it should have said here first "between appears outside" and "between appears inside" is judged because of lechi; but since in the Gemara this matter is set first "appears inside" etc. — and it is possible to impose this on Taz; and nevertheless it is difficult — it should have said the wording of Tur see there. (What Semag wrote there that the Mechaber explained "equal outside-inside" not to reduce, for it is not like asking — and therefore there letter 9; and in Klalim in the opening of the book we wrote from this.) And what Taz wrote in diagrams 70 Gemara and in Maharshal and Perishah — he expanded here and there was no time to study it properly:`,
  "peri-megadim:7:_": `If — Taz and likewise Magen Avraham letter 11 — whatever the lechi length is four amot, which is the qualification of mavo as in seif 26, and stands in the width of the mavo — leaning does not help see there; and even if he placed lechi not for the sake of permitting mavo and leaned afterward — it is nothing; only in the initial making from the start intent is required to permit; and the gloss: but if he leaned it — it should be in seif 11 here — leaning does not help as stated. See letter 13:`,
  "peri-megadim:8:_": `Lechi — Taz and likewise Magen Avraham letter 14 — that here even if fixed for the sake of lechi it is forbidden; for regarding width, because they closed it more — they did not reduce; however there we require at least that it was fixed. And what is written here implies we do not hold like Tosafos; and all the more letter 27 he brought Ra'osh's words see there — he left requires study:`,
  "peri-megadim:9:_": `And it is required — Taz: in the edition we marked in section 64, and it should be in section 66 — lechi standing by itself is lechi because of partition, and korah by itself is not korah, for for recognition we require that it was fixed for that purpose; and in seif 64 the reason that we require korah over the mavo and there is none — like Rava Eruvin 8b; and see Beit Yosef — the halacha is like Rava; and if so it is permitted to use beneath the korah — if so according to those who permit it is forbidden; and it is possible open to karmelit; and siman 364 seif 4 — and it implies he placed two pegs inside the mavo and korah on them — permitted even for Rava; and likewise seif 21 placed on the pillars — namely in a manner that the pillars are not judged because of lechi; and likewise what the Mechaber wrote a fortiori the mavo; and see Olat Shabbat on this. And siman 388:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:10:_": `Seif 26: Less than ten tefachim spaced. I am in doubt whether this general rule of the Sages' measures to be stringent with spaced and compressed is like a manner of doubt in which amot we measure and each time we take stringently; and if so when mavo is four wide metsumtsamet according to the other view 28 — its length of mavo four metsumtsamot and height nineteen metsumtsamot suffice to permit it with lechi — from necessity if we measure the mavo spaced it needs no correction since its width has no measure of four spaced; and if we measure metsumtsamot — automatically its length and height are measure; or this is from the essence of the law that where amot and tefachim are mentioned for leniency the explanation is spaced; and where stated for stringency — namely metsumtsamot; and as if the Sages explicitly said mavo four tefachim metsumtsamot requires correction with lechi and must be height nineteen spaced and length four spaced; and likewise in Kilayim fence breached in ten — namely metsumtsamot; and in such a case if distancing vineyard four metsumtsamot helps — since if we measure spaced automatically permitted because of fence since there is not eleven spaced; and it appears to me proof from Tosafos Eruvin 5:1 s.v. and if in four etc.; and even though four that R' Yosi says etc. — why did they not simply challenge that the four R' Yosi says is spaced for all barley for stringency is spaced; and if the measure of houses and courtyards open into it that opening measure four is also spaced — if so Tosafos did not challenge at all; for it is fine to say that the four R' Yosi says is literally four; and nevertheless its length is more than its width for the four is spaced and its width in four is metsumtsamot; and they challenge well: how do you find it — behold it requires open into it opening measure four spaced; perforce according to side one above; and if so we should not have challenged the three views that the four is spaced; for one may say the four R' Yosi says is according to its width four — if its width four metsumtsamot also its length in four metsumtsamot; and in the manner of from necessity as above; and they challenge well that in such a case where there are four metsumtsamot how do you find it; to this they challenged that also when its width four metsumtsamot it requires its length more than four metsumtsamot for behold it requires its length more than its width:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:11:_": `Further it appears to me proof from the sugya there from what Abbaye said: I would say from R' Huna that lechi protruding etc. and R' Yosi to remove from the law of lechi; and if it were that these measures of spaced and metsumtsamot are from the essence of the law — if so for Abbaye who removes from lechi depends on mavo measure — if lechi is mavo measure it removes from lechi; if so since mavo measure is four spaced — likewise protruding lechi should not remove from lechi until it is four spaced; and behold what R' Huna said four requires another lechi — certainly four metsumtsamot as the transmitted rule that amot for stringency are metsumtsamot; and even if we strain since he said like mavo we know it is spaced which is mavo measure; nevertheless for R' Yosi who does not tie this to mavo measure — for mavo measure is four tefachim — until four it does not remove from lechi; if so automatically four is metsumtsamot; for it is in the category of what they said all measures for stringency are metsumtsamot; and this is not implied that Rabbah and R' Yosi explicitly disagree on R' Huna's explanation of four requires another lechi; for not for Abbaye is four spaced; and for R' Yosi four is metsumtsamot; perforce according to side one above that measures are in the manner of doubt and stringently; therefore also for Abbaye that four requires another lechi is metsumtsamot; for what mavo measure four spaced is only in the manner of doubt and stringency; and if so conversely for judging it as lechi that left the law of lechi — since it has mavo measure — we also go stringently that metsumtsamot is mavo measure and requires another lechi to permit it; and in truth that other lechi suffices in height nineteen metsumtsamot from the manner of necessity that if you judge amot spaced — automatically the first lechi of four metsumtsamot has law of lechi and the mavo is permitted with one of those lechayim — examine well:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:12:_": `There in the Rama gloss: And some disagree. But with openings only through windows even when many together — nevertheless it is called two courtyards — Tosafos:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:13:_": `There: Two mashehuyin or tzurat hapetach. I question — behold tzurat hapetach that makes a reed from here and from here automatically there are two pasin — and how is it applicable to say or tzurat hapetach; for why trouble with a reed on them — requires study:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:14:_": `Magen Avraham s.k. 27: It implies he deals with enclosed by wall. See responsum Bach Chadashot siman 3:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:15:_": `Magen Avraham s.k. 35: The airspace comes. So is Rashi s.v. there is no halacha; but I wonder what this is needed for; for without this since two mavo'ot were not made the place of the pas reduces the air — nevertheless the mavo width is still more than ten amot through the two airspaces; for the airspaces are twenty amot minus the place of the pas and only through the pas the mavo is divided and becomes two mavo'ot; and behold in what they challenge afterward and why should he make pas of one amah etc. and he did not know the reasoning that airspace comes from this side etc.; and even so the pas in the middle would not help all the more; or as above; and requires study:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:16:_": `Even HaEzer 363:3 and proof for this from tractate Ohalot. To this R' Shneur Zalman intended in responsum of Shevut Yaakov part 3 siman 28:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:17:_": `Seif 34: Tzurat hapetach for the small one. It is not understood to one of small mind the reason for this; and I saw afterward in Levush who extended to give a reason; and still requires study:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:18:_": `Magen Avraham s.k. 37: And lechi is also required. So is explicitly in Yerushalmi beginning of Eruvin. And thus: he makes pas of three amot and whatever he distances from the wall two amot — all the more it is judged as lechi — end of his words; and the commentator kaf ayin there wrote from this proof not like Shulchan Aruch HaGadol that Magen Avraham brought above s.k. 5; and in responsum I expanded:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:1:_": `Seif 2: And the same if some of them remained. And even if that remainder is four wide it is not nullified from the law of pas; and I would say for below seif 12. Tosafos Eruvin daf 5 s.v. it is not the law and daf 9b s.v. wall:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:2:_": `Seif 3: Ten tefachim. Spaced as below seif 26. As it appears to me:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:3:_": `Seif 6: It is required that the lechi. I question this — for behold this is included in seif 10. And why state twice:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:4:_": `Magen Avraham s.k. 5: The airspace comes. And so it is in Levush:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:5:_": `Seif 11: Such as if there was another lechi. I am in doubt if there was a partition and it fell on Shabbat and more than a tefach remained — whether we say it helps since that part was within the partition they relied on — it is called they relied on it; for on the entire partition they relied to permit carrying. And that part is included. And it appears reasonable that specifically they relied on it as lechi is required; for we hold lechi is for partition and partition standing by itself helps. And it implies also without relied such as if there was another partition and it fell on Shabbat and they now come to rely on this partition. And perforce this that with lechi it does not help requires recognition that we consider it a partition by relying on it initially. And if so relied as lechi is required. But from the sugya in Eruvin 93b it is difficult. And if there was a small courtyard breached to a large one — and the question: if so also the large to the thirteen — why was it dealing with breach on Shabbat; if so they did not rely from yesterday on the permission of the projections; perforce it is proven it is called relied since the projections were within the partition they relied on; unless we say pasin are preferable to lechi and do not require relied on it. If so one of these is proven — either in such a case it is called relied; or with pasin we do not require relied — requires study for the law:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:6:_": `And behold in the main passage of Dicha there is room to examine: from where do we know that when there was another lechi and it fell on Shabbat it does not help — perhaps we only say they did not rely except that they did not know from yesterday about this lechi or they did not carry on the past Shabbat through this lechi as Magen Avraham wrote in s.k. 9; but in such a case where there was another lechi — one may say since we hold on Shabbat once permitted, permitted; only when lechi fell — since there are no partitions — we do not say once permitted, permitted; if so perhaps since this lechi is a partition only lacking recognition — one may say we established it as a partition and we say once permitted, permitted; and from that in Eruvin 15:1 Rav sat in that mavo — they showed him and he stood in his place — there is no proof; for Rav according to his view holds even when partitions exist we do not say once permitted, permitted; and on the contrary with this it is more spacious — that which R' Huna said: the Master does not hold one may rely on a palm trunk; R' Huna according to his view holds when partitions exist we say once permitted, permitted — one may rely on a palm trunk; and perhaps since the baraita also teaches its beams fell — we hold korah is for recognition; if so when korah fell it is called partitions exist since only recognition is lacking; perforce nevertheless since the Sages said with three partitions one is not permitted except with recognition — where recognition was removed it is as if partitions were nullified; likewise this lechi without recognition is nullified there from partition rabbinically and still requires study:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:7:_": `Seif 16: Therefore if he made it from. I question why he was silent and did not distinguish that from asherah of a gentile it helps as below siman 386 seif 3:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:8:_": `There from asherah wood — invalid. And if with korah four wide it depends on two answers in Tosafos Eruvin chapter 6 in the reason that lechi is valid with asherah wood — explanation 1: since its width is mashehu they were not strict with it — see Magen Avraham s.k. 7; and if so here with korah even if its width is four it does not help with asherah wood; but according to R' Avraham that lechi if from attached plaster chips it is lechi — see Taz s.k. 5 and this is that korah cannot be through attached plaster chips for it requires fit to receive a brick; if so with korah four wide it helps with asherah wood for even though it is cut down it is like attached plaster chips and makes from them korah of four — as it appears to me:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:9:_": `Magen Avraham s.k. 16: With this suffices the length of the korah. For with nine amot and two mashehuyin suffices the length of the korah and from two ends we say lavud as written etc. — thus it should read:`,
  "turei-zahav:12:_": `If the lechi protrudes into it four amot. Rashi explained: since the permitted length of mavo is released thereby in general and it is called mavo — likewise here even if they made it for the sake of lechi it would be valid because of its closure it is not less than lechi mashehu; here since they did not establish it for lechi in our thought we cannot remove it from the law of wall and make it lechi — since it is removed from the law of lechi it turns out there is no correction for this mavo and another lechi is required to permit the mavo — end of his words; and since Rashi stated that in thought we do not remove it from the law of mavo it implies perforce that it requires in actuality — namely making it initially for the sake of lechi and it does not help with lechi standing by itself and they relied on it on erev Shabbat; and he relied on this count even though with small lechi it is valid; and so too Tosafos that Beit Yosef brings that wrote when he placed it for the sake of lechi it has publicity and recognition; and nevertheless Tur and Shulchan Aruch wrote this law even with relying on it; and in Beit Yosef it is written that from Rashi and Tosafos it implies that with relying it is as if he placed it explicitly — and Beit Yosef left Tur's words in doubt and it is without precision: in some Shulchan Aruch it is written here Rama gloss but there was no lechi and it is a printer's error — its place is before this seif:`,
  "turei-zahav:13:_": `Lechi extending along the side of the mavo. — meaning along the length of the mavo; and in this manner the law applies even if established for lechi — for even though before this it was permitted meaning it protrudes into the width of the mavo and its closure is preferable to lechi mashehu; unlike here where it does not close and whatever four amot — there is no law of lechi even if established for lechi; but less than four amot is valid for this is visible outside and equal inside; and this law is not in Tur only in the Gemara according to Tosafos' explanation and here it implies we do not hold like Tosafos' words that we mentioned in seif 9 that we do not say in such a case visible outside and equal inside that it is lechi:`,
  "turei-zahav:15:_": `And it is required that he place it. — korah is for recognition and there is no recognition here; but lechi is for partition — therefore it helps above with standing by itself:`,
  "turei-zahav:18:א": `That are not a tefach in width. — for otherwise the supports would be considered as korah themselves and not in their length three so that the korah be alone to the wall; and this is explained in seif 25:`,
  "turei-zahav:18:ב": `That must be strong. — in Tur it is also written: if one was of straw of reeds — invalid; it appears to me he needed to write this for straw and reeds are not severed because of the weight of half a brick but because it is soft and thin it is compressed and bent and it is possible the bending reaches an invalid place and even if it does not sever entirely nevertheless invalid:`,
  "turei-zahav:1:_": `That has three partitions. In Tur it is written here that it is complete reshut ha-yachid from the Torah, and Beit Yosef questioned: behold in siman 345 Tur wrote that mavo'ot that have three partitions and no lechi and korah on the fourth side are like karmelit; and he answered that what he said here complete reshut ha-yachid is not to make one who throws into it from reshut ha-rabbim liable but to permit carrying within it etc., for such is the law of karmelit from the Torah etc., and this is strained; for behold he said here complete reshut ha-yachid; and in my humble opinion to answer: in siman 345 it deals that the fourth opening is to reshut ha-rabbim as he wrote there, and the corners adjacent to reshut ha-rabbim such as mavo'ot that have three partitions etc.; and this mavo sometimes the public press to enter there when there are many people in reshut ha-rabbim as stated in the Gemara chapter 1 of Shabbos daf 10 — therefore even though by itself it is complete reshut ha-yachid nevertheless reshut ha-rabbim nullifies it from being reshut ha-yachid but it is called side of reshut ha-rabbim and is like karmelit; unlike here where we do not deal with this matter that the public press there sometimes — it remains on it the name complete reshut ha-yachid from the Torah even though he said here wherever there are three partitions etc.; and this is in Mehadrin part 17; and the wording of Knesset HaMelekh that Tur mentioned implies even open to reshut ha-rabbim and this is where the public do not press there; and understand that wherever Tur stated it refers to courtyard and mavo:`,
  "turei-zahav:22:_": `Within a tefach. — and even though the law of lavud is up to three — here it is different: zil hakah leika shiyura and zil hakah leika shiyura:`,
  "turei-zahav:24:_": `Somewhat below ten. — in Tur likewise only the word somewhat is not written there; and Beit Yosef explained: it appears that when the korah is placed above ten it deals — but if placed below ten even if its width is not more than required — invalid as Mahari wrote etc.; and his words are not understood; for it is obvious that korah below ten is invalid; and when its width above ten goes until below ten — obviously it is valid for here one need not take anything like with bent — but all this is not the topic here that one makes a wide korah from above downward for korah qualification — this matter he is not dealing with; and nevertheless it is obvious that Beit Yosef's intent is not to tie the permission of this thing specifically to what is below ten; for certainly if above ten it is preferable; rather he said that the head of the korah must be above ten; and Mahari Chalavah disagrees with Beit Yosef and wrote that he understood that the concern is in what part of it is below ten and not above ten — and it is not so; rather one must erase the mem of above ten and it must read ten — meaning the partition is ten — and in the abbreviations he also wrote like here there is a printing error only it must read ten — that is the measure of partition — perforce his words; and in truth Beit Yosef did not intend this and heaven forbid to say it would be judged as korah more when reaching below ten than when reaching above ten; rather regarding prohibition Beit Yosef said thus that the head of the korah should not be below ten — and this is a matter that could not be written here; and the main point is there is no printing error neither in Tur nor in the abbreviations except to amplify they said thus for when the korah is above ten obviously it is valid as korah for behold korah is not nullified there because of partition for there is no partition above ten at all; and this even for Rashba is valid for he said korah that leans below nineteen is invalid — it teaches us that above nineteen is valid — perforce Ra'osh and Tur said it is not so but even if it reaches until below ten and one would say there is partition here and korah is nullified — it teaches us that korah is not nullified there because of its size since the korah is visible — all this is obvious and nothing to emend here; and I wonder at Rashba's view on this; and what Magen Avraham cited from Rashi in seif 12 regarding lechi protruding into the mavo that korah is not nullified because it is large if made for lechi initially — what difference is there here regarding korah that we should not say thus:`,
  "turei-zahav:26:א": `Spaced. — and in the conclusion metsumtsamot — and in both we go stringently:`,
  "turei-zahav:26:ב": `It is not permitted by korah. — for korah is for recognition and above twenty there is no recognition for the eye does not rule over it; but lechi helps for we hold lechi is for partition even though the mavo is very high it is not needed:`,
  "turei-zahav:26:ג": `Must hollow etc. — the reason it does not suffice with a tefach like above twenty — Rashi explained: there it is stated for the side that is already called a side only needs to reduce somewhat for slight recognition suffices; but here that now he makes it a side — less than ten is not called a side — it requires that this side be visible to the measure of mavo qualification:`,
  "turei-zahav:26:ד": `And some disagree on this. — they are Rif and Rambam who hold that since they are open to each other it is as if all are one courtyard and mavo requires two courtyards open into it; the first view is Ra'osh and Tur Mordechai and Rashba shows where he erred here:`,
  "turei-zahav:26:ה": `They have the law of courtyards. — accordingly we would need to correct a pas of four on one side or two mashehuyin on each side as above in courtyard — but it appears they are not accustomed to do so because of open mavo that requires specifically tzurat hapetach:`,
  "turei-zahav:26:ו": `And perforce one must beware etc. — and it appears since one must place reeds below at height ten, one need not tie the rope above on the reeds but may tie the rope even on the roof from this side and that, since the essence of tzurat hapetach is from the reeds below; also from the Gemara we conclude that the upper reed need not touch the reeds — if so perforce it is tied elsewhere; and one need not beware now how the rope is tied on the side of the reeds — for above siman 362 seif 11 we said it does not help — meaning there those reeds themselves are the reeds for tzurat hapetach or there are also reeds above — namely above from the roof and below other reeds — in this one must beware they are in place of doorposts and the way of an opening is only the lintel on the doorposts; unlike if above there are no reeds — only tzurat hapetach through reeds placed below at height ten for tzurat hapetach and above tied on the roof since one cannot place long reeds there from the rope to the ground — then one need not beware how it is tied above on roofs but only that they stand aligned under the rope — for behold there are no doorposts but only what is below at height ten and it is considered as if the rope rests on them literally as stated above to decide this rabbinic matter: and it appears further that if above the reeds placed below there is a roof interrupting between the height of the rope and the reeds there is prohibition in this for it is not tzurat hapetach since the roof interrupts between the rope and the reeds — and we do not make tzurat hapetach in this manner except one must open a large hole in the roof above the reeds in a manner that they are aligned under the rope without interruption; and above I wrote in seif 7 regarding lechi from smeared plaster: and it appears further that if under the rope on the wall projections extend up to ten tefachim near the ground — one may well rely on them on erev Shabbat in place of lechi reeds; and likewise the projections must be exposed above under the rope as in this matter:`,
  "turei-zahav:29:_": `Lest the sea rise as a sandbar. — it will be explained in the responsum that I answered to the community of Steinitz — this is the text of the question: in the community of Steinitz in the land of Mehrin they practiced moving objects on Shabbat without an eruv relying on the city being surrounded by a river around and they acquired permission from the lord — is it fit to do so or not. Answer: it appears it is not correct to do so for two reasons. The first is explicit in the Gemara and poskim and the second although not explicit is nevertheless clearly proven in the Gemara and poskim. The explicit reason is stated in Eruvin daf 8: R' Yehuda — an incident of a mavo one of whose sides ends at the sea and one at a public garbage heap — the matter came before Rabbi and he said neither prohibition nor permission; prohibition he did not say for partitions stand; permission he did not say lest the garbage heap be removed and the sea rise as a sandbar; Rashi explained sandbar of sand and stones at its bank for such is its way and the width of the sea shortens and ground of a parsah or more is made; Beit Yosef in Orach Chayim siman 363 in the name of Magen Avraham Meirat Einayim wrote we are concerned lest the sea rise as a sandbar; and there is dispute among poskim whether we hold this concern — as explained in siman 363 and Beit Yosef ruled to be lenient and Rama brought the stringent view — if so certainly you should be stringent in this concern lest part of the waters at its bank be covered by sand and stones and they carry there. Second: it appears here all hold to prohibition even though they said at the end of the thrower in tractate Shabbos daf 100: a pit in reshut ha-rabbim ten deep and four wide full of water — if one threw into it he is liable; full of fruits — if one threw into it he is exempt — why? water does not nullify partition; fruits nullify partition etc. — it teaches us that through water partition is not nullified; nevertheless since the way of water here is to freeze and ice in cold season — behold at those times there is no partition at all and it is obvious forbidden to carry; and this concern does not apply to sea that does not freeze; but in that of making pasin 4:22 in what Magen Avraham wrote surrounding it the Euphrates on this side: and one should not learn permission from the mishnah of chapter Window (Eruvin daf 78) that taught: a ditch between two courtyards ten deep and four wide — they make two eruvin and not one; even full of straw or hay, full of earth or stones — they make one eruv and not two etc. — it teaches us that through filling with straw partitions are not nullified since he will take the straw from there for his animal — likewise we would say since frozen water will eventually melt and become flowing water as at first partitions are not nullified; and one may say on this: perhaps here it is different since freezing happens by itself not by human action unlike filling with straw; if so perhaps it helps here what will eventually be removed; but there is no question for behold we find in chapter He May Not Dig (Bava Batra daf 20) regarding reduction of window that brings there: he removed grass and placed it in the window or grass that grew by itself in the window — it is not reduction; behold before you that growing by itself has no advantage to nullify more than coming there by human action; and likewise we may say freezing of water is considered as if the pit were filled with straw that does not nullify the pit; but all this is not so for Tosafos there in chapter Window explained that filling with straw that does not nullify partition is because that straw is taken on Shabbat and is not muktzeh; and they bring proof from what we say there afterward that even a pouch nullifies a person etc.; and that fruits nullify partition even though taken on Shabbat is d'oraisa and certainly nullifies; but for eruv which is rabbinic one may distinguish between taken on Shabbat and not taken on Shabbat — perforce we learn from this that whatever is certainly there on Shabbat — forbidden to take from there such as straw prepared for bricks not for animal feed — is called certainly nullification of partition and even though certainly afterward he will take it — nevertheless every Shabbat this is called nullification of partition; likewise here and all the more since frozen water will certainly stand all Shabbat; and further proof from chapter Window (Eruvin daf 77a): Rav Yechiel covered a bowl — meaning adjacent to the wall and reduced the height of the wall less than ten from the bowl to the head of the wall from his reduction; and they challenge there: and why — it is taken on Shabbat; and they answer: it is not needed for he attached it to the ground for that bowl and they will not move it on Shabbat; and they challenge: what difference from muktzeh that he hid in straw that is forbidden to take on Shabbat if part of it is not exposed; and they answer: it is not needed for we require recognition and a basket; behold before us that specifically what is fit to take on that Shabbat is not considered to nullify partition; but not what is forbidden to take on Shabbat even though certainly he will take it after Shabbat through recognition and basket — nevertheless now it is considered nullification of partition; if so all the more here since freezing is accustomed to stand many weeks on the water; and further proof for prohibition from the initial enactment that the Rabbis enacted recognition in lechi and korah for closed mavo from three sides — Rashi explained at the beginning of Eruvin in the first mishnah: the Rabbis decreed in mavo lest they confuse with complete reshut ha-rabbim; and likewise in karmelit the Rabbis decreed on them lest they confuse with reshut ha-yachid as stated chapter 1 of Shabbos daf 6; and all our domains are karmelit as all poskim wrote — if so here when water freezes and people walk there like in the rest of the city and sometimes snow covers until the water underneath is not recognizable at all — it is obvious the Rabbis decreed more lest they confuse with reshut ha-rabbim for there is no recognition at all here. And one may say: only on those Shabbatot when water freezes would carrying be forbidden in the entire city; but on other Shabbatot not — this is not so for we said in that of concerned lest sea rise sandbar that I brought above that Rabbi did not wish to say permission even though partitions stand from the reason lest sometimes there be no partition there — all the more here that most of the year certainly there is decree even all year because of those Shabbatot; and further proof from mishnah of chapter Window (Eruvin daf 78) regarding ditch — if one placed a board four wide — if they wish they make one eruv; and from this one may not learn permission; for if they freeze in rainy season partition is nullified and forbidden to carry; and see in responsum of Chacham Tzvi siman 37; (and see in Even HaEzer who expanded to prove frozen water does not nullify partitions — see there in Maginei Eretz printing Propes): however if on the riverbank on dry land there is a slope ten high over four amot as written siman 366 — we consider that slope in place of a partition, end of his words:`,
  "turei-zahav:2:א": `A courtyard fully breached etc. — meaning square; but mavo its length is more than its width:`,
  "turei-zahav:2:ב": `A tefach from here. — it implies there is a difference between making a pas — then two mashehuyin suffice as he mentioned first; but in what remained a tefach is required and this was not mentioned in Beit Yosef — on the contrary he wrote the opposite on the words of R' Yehuda; except that Tosafos chapter 1 daf 10 wrote to distinguish thus; end of his words; and Beit Yosef himself brought this afterward:`,
  "turei-zahav:30:_": `Except opposite the short side. — meaning it is forbidden to place it diagonally and use the long side — Rava holds korah diagonally is not recognition; Rashi explained: those viewing the people of the mavo extend and use reshut ha-rabbim outside except opposite the short wall; or: permitted to use reshut ha-rabbim according to what is not like that part of the mavo — end of his words; and nevertheless the reason appears permitted diagonally within the mavo since there one will not err in permission in reshut ha-rabbim; and this is specifically when there is not more than ten in the diagonal for the Gemara said when more than ten all hold he does not place opposite the short but diagonally; but within ten we hold like Rava who said opposite the short; and accordingly within the mavo Rava also permits diagonally — this also within ten thus appeared initially; except Shulchan Aruch and Tur wrote but if he wishes he may make tzurat hapetach diagonally within the mavo — he may; it implies within the mavo equals tzurat hapetach and tzurat hapetach even in width more than ten; and Beit Yosef wrote on outside the mavo that tzurat hapetach helps and it appears initially likewise for lechayim for we hold lechi is for partition; and straightly; but two lechayim placed one opposite the other diagonally — permitted to use until opposite the lechayim diagonally provided there is not more than ten between the lechayim etc.; and it is possible that even though lechayim are for partition they do not help like tzurat hapetach etc. — end of his words; and Mahari Chalavah wrote plainly that when there is not more than ten lechayim help as explained shortly afterward; but if they made in the middle two pasin etc. — it teaches that similar to tzurat hapetach; and what Raavad wrote here that tzurat hapetach helps even in width ten — meaning to say even in width ten it helps unlike two lechayim; and not like Beit Yosef's conclusion that lechayim do not help — and it is an error regarding him for certainly they help even diagonally — end of his words; and in my humble opinion even doubt there is none as Beit Yosef thought; rather it is simple they do not help at all for whenever lechayim do not stand equally they are not this matter to each other; that one standing at the short side itself permits carrying in the mavo until opposite it straight and no more outward; and that lechi standing at the long side neither helps nor hurts for it does not permit opposite it for behold opposite it is only reshut ha-rabbim; if so it is only like an ordinary tree and what would the lechi opposite the short help him and it has no partnership with him and the short side does not need it; unlike korah placed diagonally from this to that — the partnership is visible to the eyes that one goes from this to that; unlike two lechayim where the one outside does not need its fellow and permits opposite itself well alone and it alone for partition — we view as if a partition spread equally opposite it and the partition has no connection with the second; and I would say regarding what is written shortly that two lechayim permit in the middle of the mavo — there they stand straight and each needs its fellow in order to divide domains and there specifically two are required as written shortly; unlike here diagonally and even within the mavo permitted here with korah diagonally — nevertheless if one made two pasin diagonally there is no partnership but that closer to outside itself permits opposite itself and the second is without need — all this appears clear and there is not even doubt for permission:`,
  "turei-zahav:31:א": `If there is a courtyard etc. — for then the people of that courtyard forbid into the mavo on those who wish to make eruv for themselves; and if the outer ones did not make lechi at the side of reshut ha-rabbim they were not fit to make eruv at all with the inner one since the mavo was not qualified from their side — they cannot forbid the others for one does not forbid one another unless all are one domain and they can make eruv together:`,
  "turei-zahav:31:ב": `But if they made in the middle etc. — this is not regarding permission to carry inward; if so one lechi suffices as at the head of the mavo; rather they came here to divide domains so the outer ones separate from the inner and do not forbid them — tzurat hapetach or two pasin is required; meaning mashehu from here and mashehu from here and it is as if an actual partition between them; for specifically for permission to carry one suffices not for dividing domains — thus Ra'osh's reasoning here:`,
  "turei-zahav:33:א": `And he places it in the middle. — meaning in the middle of the opening:`,
  "turei-zahav:33:ב": `And every condition of mavo etc. — thus it should read and not as some books wrote condition of courtyard:`,
  "turei-zahav:33:ג": `And what is from the pas until the wall etc. — in Hagahot Ashiri and Tur it is written if there is from the pas to the middle wall of the mavo more than ten amot etc.; and Beit Yosef wrote I do not know why he wrote thus for even if there is not more than ten between them its law is like crooked mavo that its law is like open — and see siman 365 seif 2; and I wonder at Ra'osh how he was silent and at Rabbeinu and R' Yeruham who did not notice this — end of his words; and nevertheless Shulchan Aruch did not write here more than ten like Tur's wording but Rama wrote it in the wording of some say; and Mahari Chalavah resolved Tur's words with great strain and I wondered at Beit Yosef's wonder on this for certainly in actual crooked mavo as in siman 364 whose form is thus — there we say that even if there is not more than ten in its bends its law is like open — for Rav and such we hold; unlike here where we make one mavo going equally as two mavo'ot — namely through pas of four in length four amot — and we have to say these two mavo'ot go equally until the middle wall and here there is no crooked mavo at all for the opening open downward from the pas is an opening from one mavo to its fellow — if so its law is like two mavo'ot one to another and there is an opening from one to its fellow — perforce they wrote if there is more than ten from the pas to the middle wall as Tosafos wrote in Beit Yosef that wrote if from the head of the pas of the inner side until the middle wall there is more than ten — tzurat hapetach is required for it is like crooked mavo etc.; behold it is explained that thereby since there is no law of opening on it it becomes crooked mavo; unlike crooked mavo in siman 364 whose form and building was literally in the bend and need not make it crooked; and I wondered Beit Yosef did not notice this for it is a simple matter in my humble opinion; and if so here too it is fit to emend thus that there is more than ten from the pas to the wall like Tur:`,
  "turei-zahav:34:_": `And he makes a pas three amot wide etc. — the rule in this is that the pas must be greater than the airspace between it and the wall; if not the airspace of this side and that side comes and nullifies the pas:`,
  "turei-zahav:36:_": `Within four amot. — meaning within four amot there is height ten — it is as reshut ha-yachid; but if within five — behold it is like other reshut ha-rabbim for its use is for walking:`,
  "turei-zahav:7:_": `And therefore they make lechi from smeared plaster etc. — and even though in Terumat HaDeshen he wrote that lechi that if wind blows on it cannot stand is invalid — and here certainly the plaster cannot stand — there it deals with lechi that is valid by itself for mavo without tzurat hapetach that requires some measure; but here what they make from smeared plaster is for tzurat hapetach that requires only one reed from here and one reed from here suffices in whatever amount; and this is the difference in Terumat HaDeshen siman 74; and accordingly Rama's words are not resolved that wrote and therefore they make lechi etc. for it implies he deals with the law of lechi without tzurat hapetach and this is not so; and below siman 362 seif 26 it is explained explicitly that this refers to tzurat hapetach that we make. And in that responsum: nevertheless it is astonishing that he saw that smearing plaster smeared on the wall sometimes comes to ruin because dogs are accustomed to rub against walls and erase the plaster smearing from the walls below more than three toward the ground and it is no longer fit for lechi nor partition for it is a partition that goats penetrate — end of his words; it implies that in any case in this smearing there must be some actual substance and somewhat thick and it does not suffice with the appearance of plaster visible on the wall alone; and so too in that responsum initially that at least it must be thick like a cloak cord; and so too Beit Yosef in the name of responsum of Ra'osh that part of it must protrude — I wrote this because I saw one teacher who commanded to mark on the wall in place of lechi with a type of white earth called kreid — and he erred greatly in this, especially in this matter of plaster smearing that does not fit the law well as Terumat HaDeshen himself wrote there that he strained to uphold the practice in this; and do not add to it — there is only folly and nonsense in this:`,
  "turei-zahav:8:_": `From asherah wood — valid. Beit Yosef wrote: and according to Maharam who holds that cloak cord specifically — do not question from that we validate asherah wood for cut down its measure; one answer: that which requires cloak cord is not because it requires measure but because it must be fit to place something on it — and asherah is also fit to place on it etc.; another answer: the reason of cut down measure is because it stands for burning and since it is possible that it will burn and become ash if one kneads and makes from it a pillar it helps as lechi — end of his words; and I am astonished that a whole mishnah at the end of Temurah was hidden from him: all that are burned — their ash is permitted except asherah wood; and as written in Yoreh De'ah siman 142 that ash of asherah is forbidden in benefit — if so one must scatter it to the wind and the ash is like the wood itself:`,
  "turei-zahav:9:_": `Whether the protrusion is visible to those inside etc. — in the Gemara it is stated first this matter: visible to those inside and equal outside, and likewise the reverse — it is judged as lechi; and Rashi explained: visible inside — he placed the lechi its width toward the length of the mavo and did not draw somewhat of its width outward but equalized its outer edge to the thickness of the walls of the mavo and is like one who adds to the width of the walls and is not visible from outside as lechi but its inner edge is visible inside; visible outside and equal inside — meaning he drew all the lechi outward and the inner edge enters the walls of the mavo and is not visible inside but only appears like adding to the length of the wall but from outside it is recognizable that it is not from the wall of the mavo since it extends beyond the width of the wall etc.; and afterward a baraita of R' Chiyya is stated: a wall one of whose sides is indented from its fellow — whether visible outside and equal inside or the reverse — it is judged as lechi; and Rashi explained another matter: indented — the building receded and reduced its width — now it protrudes and extends like a kind of pillar called piler — whether its indentation is visible inside etc. that the wall recedes behind it and the inner part is visible inside; whether visible outside and equal inside such as the wall recedes from before it so the indentation is not visible to those behind it but relative to reshut ha-rabbim — end of his words; and Beit Yosef wrote regarding Rashi's second explanation: it must read that the side of the wall that was toward the mavo is called behind the wall and the side of the wall outside the mavo is called the face of the wall — end of his words; and the explanation is very astonishing: why should the side of the hollow of the mavo be called behind the wall; for Tosafos question on Rashi's first explanation that if visible outside is considered when the lechi is toward the inner side — how do they answer below that small walls enter etc.; and according to Beit Yosef's explanation in the second explanation it would also question on the second explanation; perforce it appears the difference is between the explanations in this: according to the first explanation it would be visible inside like this ט and visible outside like this ם — it turns out in both the lechi is not at the edge of the wall on the side of the mavo for this does not help as Tosafos' question; rather they agree with the second explanation and it is in this manner: the face of the wall is called that side facing reshut ha-rabbim — namely the very head of the wall; and behind the wall are called the sides in the wall; perforce visible inside is when there is a flaw in behind the wall — namely the side toward the mavo like this ט — and it is exactly like the first explanation and visible outside is when there is a flaw in the face of the wall that is the upper head like this ח — this helps specifically since the lechi is not toward the hollow of the mavo for then there is not much recognition but appears like a long wall; and this is explained in Mordechai that wrote there are two explanations in Rashi on visible outside; and according to Beit Yosef there would also be two explanations in visible inside of Rashi's explanation; but certainly as I explained thus Rashi is correct; and I also saw other paths; and in my humble opinion I wrote; and see seif 13:`,
  "yad-ephraim:10:_": `There: and it appears etc. — projections extend to nineteen near ground — perhaps he meant the projections begin near ground and go in height until ten tefachim; and perhaps printing error and must read projections nineteen until near ground; and in Mishbetzot Zahav it is written that it appears to him to emend until three — see there:`,
  "yad-ephraim:11:_": `There: in place of reeds of tzurat hapetach and also etc. — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:12:_": `In Magen Avraham s.k. 37: and what Magen Avraham wrote — see siman 364 that on the other side two pasin suffice; in Sefer Tosafot Shabbos he expanded his words that here two pasin are required like courtyard and the sea questioned Darkhei Moshe for it implies tzurat hapetach is required and wrote perhaps specifically in complete mavo Magen Avraham speaks that lechi suffices; but here tzurat hapetach is required — end of his words; and this is not Magen Avraham's intent; and in my humble opinion Magen Avraham holds that even here lechi suffices; for even if it has law of courtyard nevertheless its breach is in ten and then tzurat hapetach is required; and if not two pasin suffice if four remained in one place or a tefach from here as explained above seif 2; and in our towns where on the fourth side also there are houses from this and that side — two pasin suffice when breach is not in ten and only lechi or korah is required because it has law of mavo and also in mavo law lechi or korah does not help if breached more than ten; and what Darkhei Moshe wrote I do not know the meaning; but I saw Darkhei Moshe wondered at Rashba that Bach brought and wrote that in courtyard tzurat hapetach does not help from here and lechi or korah from here but two pasin are required from the other side; and in my humble opinion the question on Rashba is when there is no breach in full and it is not in ten only open from two sides to reshut ha-rabbim where it is — then Rashba speaks well that tzurat hapetach is a complete partition and is not less than mavo where the other side is permitted with lechi or korah; and what is in the name of Maharil in responsum requires study; for Maharil siman 262 and Bach brought that he doubts perhaps lechi alone does not suffice — this is when open to gentiles' mavo'ot and from reason of removing residents as explained in his wording explicitly there; but regarding mavo qualification he agrees lechi alone suffices — see there; see in Tosafot who wrote with lechi or korah from here and even though lechi does not help in width ten — one may say he makes a wide lechi that reduces the hollow to ten etc.; and it must read that even though open to reshut ha-rabbim namely sixteen amot nevertheless the mavo itself is not wide sixteen only before the mavo there is reshut ha-rabbim from this and that; and what Tosafot wrote there daf 6 and daf 59 and see above siman 345 and in responsum I expanded on this matter:`,
  "yad-ephraim:13:_": `There in s.k. 31 that Eimimar ruled in Sura in his responsum etc. — initially one may say that the mavo's correction is recognition lest one carry from there to the place of the sandbar, and so too Eliyah Rabbah; but below in siman 365 seif 5 it is stated: one should not sit at the head of the mavo with an object in his hand etc. — it appears this is not recognition lest one carry from reshut ha-rabbim there; and that which we say korah is for recognition does not mean recognition lest one carry from the mavo to reshut ha-rabbim etc. — rather recognition lest one come to carry in reshut ha-rabbim itself — see Rashi daf 3; and nevertheless there in siman 365 s.k. 11 Magen Avraham wrote it is possible tzurat hapetach is recognition regarding this concern lest one carry the object taken from his hand to reshut ha-yachid into the mavo; and if so here too tzurat hapetach would help; and as Magen Avraham wrote from Eimimar who made a complete partition; and it is possible Eimimar could not make a low tzurat hapetach because they needed to water camels through there — and similarly Tosafos wrote on daf 105 regarding let him make tzurat hapetach at the mouth of the path for camels come etc. — see there; and therefore he ruled in reshut ha-rabbim that he could easily remove it — as the Rav HaMaggid wrote — see there; and a high tzurat hapetach has no recognition concern as Magen Avraham wrote there s.k. 11 in siman 365; and accordingly what Magen Avraham wrote and tzurat hapetach does not help — meaning high specifically; but a low tzurat hapetach has recognition concern according to Magen Avraham himself whom I brought — requires study:`,
  "yad-ephraim:14:_": `There in that seif tzurat hapetach even if it is etc. — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:15:_": `In Taz s.k. 21 that he does not place diagonally except opposite the short — only regarding ten they dispute and we rule like Rava etc. — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:16:_": `In Shulchan Aruch seif 31: there is a courtyard from the lechi outward etc. — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:17:_": `Magen Avraham s.k. 34: except at one opening the entire city is permitted with these partitions — that opening's etc. — and one may qualify half — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:18:_": `Seif 36 in the gloss: and with this the mound counts as a partition — the language is garbled and explained for one who examines Beit Yosef — the Tur wrote that it rises in place of lechi and Beit Yosef wrote that according to lechi sufficing he wrote thus; but in truth the slope rises in place of a partition; but in any case there is no difference here whether it is like lechi or like a partition; and if so why is he precise and with this the mound counts as a partition; and what Beit Yosef wrote but the mound certainly that if it rose only in place of lechi it would not help at all since there are no partitions — rather the reason is because it rises in place of a partition for we say gud asik — end of his words; his intent is not that here regarding mavo qualification it would not help at all from the Torah it would not help since mavo is qualified by lechi; and even though lechi itself is for partition — nevertheless the mound that is ten high is like lechi and gud asik — why do I need; rather their intent is that there it discusses slope and mound and regarding mound we say if one threw and it landed on it he is liable; and on this Beit Yosef says if the mound had no other elevation only that it is like lechi it would not help at all — meaning for liability as he brought there before from Talmud and Rambam; and it is possible Beit Yosef is missing and must read it would not help at all to make him liable on it; and on this he says well since there are no partitions — meaning and if so what of it that it landed on it for behold there are no partitions around — only we say gud asik; thus appears Beit Yosef's explanation initially; and Rama's wording that wrote and with this etc. — it appears specifically in this we say it counts as a partition — requires study; and also here it deals only with slope and not mound; and even though Tur wrote in its wording that the mound rises in place of lechi and Beit Yosef wrote that for slope too they call mound for behold it is like a mound of earth — nevertheless Tur deals also with a complete mound — see there — and wrote mound including both; unlike here where it does not deal with mound at all; and why did he leave slope and write mound; and lest I fear a printing error fell in Rama and must read and likewise mound counts as a partition and the scribe erred and wrote in place of likewise with this and also separated the two heh of likewise and added by hand the word and with this the mound and this is a possible and common error; and his words revolve around what Beit Yosef wrote that in the Talmud mound was not mentioned for mavo only for throwing and Tosafot wrote mound also for mavo for they hold no difference and Rambam did not mention it only for throwing and not for not needing lechi — see there; and Rama intended to bring this law of Tosafot since the Mechaber wrote only Rambam's wording that mentioned only slope and not mound; and on this he wrote that likewise mound is as a partition; and nevertheless in Darkhei Moshe it is stated on Tosafot's words that the mound rises in place of lechi and Beit Yosef wrote and not in place of lechi but rises in place of a complete partition and so is proven from the Gemara — end of his words; it appears he also intended this in the gloss since he did not mention in Darkhei Moshe that word Beit Yosef wrote that even though only slope was mentioned not mound — Tur holds likewise mound; and nevertheless it is not far to say that here in the gloss he comes to write rulings and wrote this word that also with mound one need not lechi or korah:`,
  "yad-ephraim:1:_": `There in Taz s.k. 4: we see in this correction of smeared plaster etc. — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:2:_": `There: for lechi and not for tzurat hapetach for it is partition etc. — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:3:_": `In Magen Avraham s.k. 5: see siman 34 — thus it should read; meaning there in s.k. 37 he brought Tosafos' words that another lechi is required for pas does not help even though the pas is very wide and stands more than the breach since it protrudes three — see there as explained there:`,
  "yad-ephraim:4:_": `Taz s.k. 13: One need not take anything for korah qualification of this matter we deal with like with bent where what bent is invalid — rather we view what is within ten as if it is not; and all this is not the topic here that one makes a wide korah from above and leaning below; and nevertheless it is obvious etc. — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:5:_": `There in that s.k. below ten specifically — meaning that even though we would say Beit Yosef's view that if placed below ten it is invalid — meaning this would cause the permission; obviously this is invalid; rather Beit Yosef only said its head is above only that it is wide and extends somewhat below; but what Taz wrote on Bach and heaven forbid to say etc. — it appears he understood what Bach wrote that the concern is etc. — meaning concern to permit that one does not permit except within ten; and this is as Beit Yosef stated in his first explanation as above; and in truth Bach's intent in what he wrote that the concern is on the concern for prohibition — meaning Beit Yosef explains Rashba teaches us that leaning somewhat below is also valid lest one think otherwise that only if all is above ten is permitted — and he teaches us that even in such a case it is valid; and on this Bach objected that Rashba deals with a matter where the korah itself is wide ten tefachim and Taz's words require study:`,
  "yad-ephraim:6:_": `There: and not all above ten and it is not so etc. that could not be written and the main point there is no printing error etc. — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:7:_": `Magen Avraham s.k. 27: One should note the law of courtyard is not combined and requires etc. small paths between house and house etc. and tzurat hapetach is not required for this reason — it appears etc. as Magen Avraham wrote at the beginning of siman 364 in openings first — thus it should read:`,
  "yad-ephraim:8:_": `There: law of one who comes to divide etc. — meaning as explained in siman 392; and see below s.k. 34 and what Magen Avraham wrote if the mavo is not wide ten amot it is a printing error and must read more than ten amot; for if it is wide more than ten amot tzurat hapetach is specifically required; and what Magen Avraham wrote is only removal of residents — meaning since enclosed by wall there is no prohibition of carrying only because it is not combined that gentiles' mavo'ot open there and correction is needed there to remove gentiles' residents and two lechayim suffice as written; and from this reason also tzurat hapetach is not required in its bend since we say its law is like courtyard; and what Maharil wrote in responsum etc. and therefore two pasin are required etc. — meaning Maharil holds even in enclosed by wall it has no law of courtyard only karmelit law because gentiles have right of passage; and even though Maharil also agrees that two pasin or tzurat hapetach help to remove residents — nevertheless as long as residents are not removed it has law of karmelit and enclosure of wall does not make it reshut ha-yachid like uncombined courtyard; and Mahari Weil holds its law is like courtyard and the difference regarding broken on Shabbat as explained at end of siman 365 and automatically difference also for crooked mavo; and see what I wrote in pamphlet on responsum of Bach Chadashot siman 80:`,
  "yad-ephraim:9:_": `Taz s.k. 19: To tie the rope from the side etc. — thus it should read:`,
};
